\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Jobless Claims Fall Amid High Demand for Workers\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sarah Chaney Cambon \par \uc2 615 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 November 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 U.S. jobless claims are gradually drifting down toward pre-pandemic levels as employers avoid layoffs and many workers quit or remain sidelined in a tight labor market.\par \par \uc2 Initial claims for jobless benefits edged down to seasonally adjusted 268,000 last week from a revised 269,000 a week earlier, the Labor Department said Thursday. Claims are at the lowest level since the pandemic hit the U.S. economy last spring.\par \par \uc2 Though worker filings for unemployment benefits remain above their 2019 weekly average of 218,000, the decline in claims has been much faster than after the 2007-2009 recession. Claims, a proxy for layoffs, took about 1.5 years to fall below 300,000 after the economy began to recover from the Covid-19 pandemic. By comparison, they took roughly five years to cross that threshold after the recession ended in mid-2009.\par \par \uc2 Continuing claims, which provide an approximation for the number of people receiving regular state benefits, fell to a pandemic low of 2.08 million in the week ended Nov. 6 from 2.21 million a week earlier.\par \par \uc2 Some individuals are moving off of unemployment benefits because they are no longer eligible to receive them. Others are likely finding jobs.\par \par \uc2 Employer demand for workers is unusually strong, economists say. Job openings are near record highs, but many businesses say they can't find enough workers to fill the roles.\par \par \uc2 High worker turnover is one challenge for employers. Though many companies are trying to expand their workforces, they are simultaneously losing workers, said \uc2 Evan Sohn\uc2 , chief executive at \uc2 Recruiter.com\uc2 , a hiring and recruiting platform.\par \par \uc2 The quits rate -- a measurement of workers leaving jobs as a share of overall employment -- was 3% in September, a record high, Labor Department figures tracing back to 2000 show.\par \par \uc2 Workers are feeling emboldened to leave their jobs because they are more valuable in today's labor market, there is less stigma about leaving a company than in the past and remote technology allows for a quicker, easier interview process, Mr. Sohn said. "This job-hopper economy is not going backward," he said.\par \par \uc2 Recruiters reported that about 53% of the roles they were filling in October were backfill roles -- or openings because of worker departures -- up from 44% in August, according to \uc2 Recruiter.com\uc2 . Recruitment for backfill positions exceeded recruitment for new jobs last month, one piece of evidence that employers are struggling with employee resignations.\par \par \uc2 Labor shortages also reflect a smaller available pool of workers amid school and child-care disruptions, as well as some workers' continued fear of the coronavirus. The labor force, or number of Americans working or seeking work, was down by nearly three million in October of this year from February 2020.\par \par \uc2 Even among those who are seeking work, several factors continue to keep some people from searching more urgently. Care responsibilities, Covid-19 fears and employed partners were the top reasons unemployed people didn't search harder for a job in October, according to jobs website \uc2 Indeed.com\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Federal unemployment benefits that expanded the amount, duration and eligibility of benefits during the pandemic have kept some people from taking jobs. Those benefits ended nationwide in September.\par \par \uc2 Still, pandemic-related continuing unemployment claims for gig workers and others not traditionally eligible for benefits rose at the end of October, potentially reflecting backlogs. Pandemic-related claims in California drove the sharp increase, economists noted.\par \par \uc2 The employment-cost index, a measure of worker compensation that includes wages and benefits, rose 1.3% in the third quarter from the second, the fastest pace since at least 2001, the Labor Department reported.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw262\pich442\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3930\pichgoal6630
