\par\par \b \uc2 Deere Workers Ratify Six-Year Contract\b0\par\par\uc2 By Bob Tita \par \uc2 486 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 November 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Workers at \uc2 Deere & Co\uc2 . ratified a new six-year contract Wednesday, ending a strike against the farm and construction machinery company that lasted over a month.\par \par \uc2 Deere said some workers will return to their plants as soon as the overnight shift early Thursday. Workers voted on a third contract offer crafted by negotiators late last week after two prior offers failed.\par \par \uc2 "We're giving employees the opportunity to earn wages and benefits that are the best in our industries," Deere Chief Executive \uc2 John May\uc2  said. "We have faith that, in return, our employees will find new and better ways to improve our competitiveness."\par \par \uc2 The offer that prevailed grafted an increase in base production pay onto the general raises, bonuses and improvements in pension funding from an offer rejected Nov. 2. Deere said following that vote that it wouldn't bargain further on regular wages and bonuses and urged workers to reconsider their opposition to the offer, which Deere said would cost it $3.5 billion.\par \par \uc2 Workers walked off their jobs Oct. 14 in the first strike against Deere in 35 years. In turning down earlier proposals, striking employees said Deere should provide bigger raises and benefit expansions when the company's equipment sales are the strongest in years. Deere has forecast about $5.8 billion in income for its full fiscal year, compared with $2.8 billion in 2020 and $3.3 billion in 2019.\par \par \uc2 "Our members' willingness to strike in order to attain a better standard of living and a more secure retirement resulted in a groundbreaking contract and sets a new standard for workers," said \uc2 Chuck Browning\uc2 , director of the United Auto Workers agricultural implement department.\par \par \uc2 Deere is one of several large U.S. companies confronted with striking workers lately as a tight labor market and rising inflation encourage workers to push for higher wages and better benefits. Workers also have conducted strikes at snack-food company \uc2 Mondelez International Inc\uc2 ., commercial-truck maker Volvo and breakfast-cereal company \uc2 Kellogg Co\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Based in Moline, Ill., Deere said workers at 14 plants will be returning to work. With the strike over, the company can shift its focus to increasing production for a bulging backlog of orders for next year's growing season.\par \par \uc2 Deere workers returning to assembly plants and warehouses will get an immediate 10% raise and each worker will receive an $8,500 bonus. Additional 5% pay raises will be provided in 2023 and 2025, and lump-sum bonuses amounting to 3% of workers' annual pay will be awarded in the three other years.\par \par \uc2 The deal also will increase the base pay level for Deere's continuous-improvement program by about 4%, giving workers more weekly pay from the program if their productivity meets the company's goals. About two-thirds of UAW-represented Deere workers receive production-based compensation on top of their regular wages.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020211118ehbi00024\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
