\par\par \b \uc2 OFF DUTY --- Gear & Gadgets -- The Fixer: Should Job Seekers Cyberstalk Their Interviewers?\b0\par\par\uc2 By Rachel Wolfe \par \uc2 445 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 November 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par D6\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Q: I have a job interview soon and am tempted to madly research the hiring manager online in case I can unearth details about her to use to my advantage when we talk. Bad idea?\par \par \uc2 A: Not entirely. I don't know about "madly," but this kind of research can come in handy if you exercise restraint. When I spotted a seemingly handmade vase in a potential employer's office during a job interview a few years ago, for example, I faced a dilemma. Thanks to some thorough \uc2 Instagram\uc2  stalking, I knew he was an avid amateur potter. But would an offhand compliment about his craftsmanship endear me to the person who held my fate in his hands -- or totally weird him out?\par \par \uc2 "There's a border between being informed and being creepy," said Los Angeles career counselor Linda Greenfield. "Saying, 'I can't help but notice the unusual piece of pottery behind you,' could be part of an icebreaker. But I wouldn't disclose that you've seen on \uc2 Instagram\uc2  that this person is a pottery expert."\par \par \uc2 That line between informed and creepy has never been blurrier at a time when, thanks to social media, it's a cinch to learn that your interlocutor not only likes dogs, but has springer spaniels named Chip and Dip. That said, some social media is required reading, said Manhattan career coach Lynn Berger: "Looking at [your interviewer's] \uc2 LinkedIn\uc2  is important, because that's a window into the job." Ms. Greenfield even recommends browsing in "public" rather than "private" mode so interviewers get an alert that you took a peek. Far from making them feel watched, she said, "you're showing them you're doing your homework." Use what you learn about their current projects and career trajectory to inform the questions you'll ask.\par \par \uc2 Gleaning talking points from \uc2 Facebook\uc2  or \uc2 Twitter\uc2  is riskier. If you're interviewing with a designer, "\uc2 Instagram\uc2  is an essential demonstration of their work," Ms. Greenfield said. If not, tread lightly. Even if you're convinced a mutual love of long-distance running is your ticket to a dream accounting role, find a way to slip your recent marathon into conversation without revealing that you know the interviewer ran it, too. Or say nothing. "There's plenty of time to get to know people naturally, through the interview process," Ms. Berger said.\par \par \uc2 In case you're wondering, I squelched my impulse to compliment the pottery. But a few months later, I left my office White Elephant holiday party with one of my now-boss's handmade bowls.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020211113ehbd00008\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
