\par\par \b \uc2 Business News: Deere Strike Stirs Farmers' Concerns\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jesse Newman and Bob Tita \par \uc2 651 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 November 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 A weekslong strike at \uc2 Deere & Co\uc2 . has dealers bracing for delayed deliveries of new equipment, and farmers fearing higher prices ahead.\par \par \uc2 Supplies of new tractors and combines across the Farm Belt have been stretched for months as manufacturers struggle with shortages of raw materials, components and semiconductor chips. Now, farmers and dealers worry that shipments from Deere, the largest seller of farm equipment in North America, will be further undermined after more than 10,000 union workers walked off their jobs Oct. 14. Members of the \uc2 United Auto Workers union\uc2  this past week rejected a second proposed contract, deepening uncertainty during the fall harvest season.\par \par \uc2 Farmer Dale Reimers, who grows corn, soybeans and other crops across 25,000 acres in eastern North Dakota, said he ordered three Deere tractors and two crop sprayers before the strike. Delivery for some equipment has been delayed for months, and it is unclear when the rest of his order will be built, he said. Late delivery of his sprayers could cut into his crop production next year, Mr. Reimers said, because his older equipment can't apply weed-killing chemicals to fields with the same precision as the new machines.\par \par \uc2 "That's going to have a big impact on profitability and production results on the dirt level," said Mr. Reimers.\par \par \uc2 Moline, Ill.-based Deere declined to comment on equipment inventories and deliveries. Deere has said it would continue operating its U.S. plants with supervisors and other nonunion employees, and that the company is considering sourcing replacement parts and machinery from its overseas plants. Dealers said they have been getting some equipment from Deere factories as nonunion workers put the finishing touches on machinery that was parked at assembly plants waiting for parts to arrive.\par \par \uc2 U.S. farmers are riding a rebound in the agricultural economy this year, fueled by a rally in prices for major commodities such as corn and soybeans. That is pushing up incomes for farmers and boosting markets for land and equipment, though rising production expenses are cutting into profits, farmers say.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 U.S. Department of Agriculture\uc2  in September projected net farm income would surge 20% this year to $113 billion, the highest since 2013. It said farm production expenses would increase by more than 7%.\par \par \uc2 The reinvigorated farm economy is driving demand for new equipment. Over the first three quarters of Deere's current fiscal year, its sales climbed 27% over the prior year, and net income more than doubled to $4.7 billion. Deere expects to earn about $5.8 billion for the full year. Deere's shares Monday closed up 1.6% at $360.86.\par \par \uc2 Union members have said the raises and expanded benefits Deere offered aren't sufficient when sales are booming and other companies are boosting wages and offering new perks to draw workers. Deere this past week said the contract rejected Nov. 2 would have represented a $3.5 billion investment in its employees over six years, and was the company's best and final offer.\par \par \uc2 Farmers' demand for equipment has remained strong in 2021, and new equipment shortages led farmers to the used market. Dealers said a prolonged strike will increase prices for used machines.\par \par \uc2 "It's a really bullish market for used equipment," said \uc2 Tom Sloan\uc2 , chairman of \uc2 Sloan Implement Co\uc2 . in Illinois. "Normally, we have too much."\par \par \uc2 Mr. Sloan said some buyers who normally turn over their older equipment to dealers as trade-ins are keeping their equipment, as the strike adds to uncertainty over the delivery of their new machines.\par \par \uc2 Deere dealers say the strike hasn't caused their customers to defect to rival brands because competitors remain constrained by their own supply and production problems. \uc2 CNH Industrial NV\uc2 , the maker of CaseIH equipment, pared its 2021 sales guidance on Thursday because of continuing supply-chain problems.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020211109ehb90000m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
