\par\par \b \uc2 Deere Workers Reject Contract Offer\b0\par\par\uc2 By Bob Tita \par \uc2 533 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 November 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Deere & Co\uc2 . workers rejected a second contract offer, extending a strike against the farm-equipment and construction-machinery company that has lasted nearly three weeks.\par \par \uc2 Union employees at 12 Deere facilities voted against the tentative contract offer agreed to Oct. 30 by their negotiators, the company said late Tuesday. Deere said employees at two parts plants in Denver and Atlanta, who work under a separate contract, voted to approve the offer that had identical economic terms as the one that the company's other employees rejected.\par \par \uc2 Deere said its investment in the employees under the failed proposal would have cost the company an additional $3.5 billion.\par \par \uc2 "Even though it would have created greater competitive challenges within our industries, we had faith in our employees' ability to sharpen our competitive edge," said \uc2 Marc Howze\uc2 , Deere's chief administrative officer." Deere will continue to serve customers in time-sensitive and critical industries and maintain operations at its plants with nonunion employees, a company spokeswoman said.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 United Auto Workers union\uc2 , which represents Deere production workers, said it would be discussing its next move in the negotiations. Union members have been urging their negotiators to broaden the scope of the bargaining to include work rules, scheduling and other compensation that workers say have eroded in recent years and weren't adequately addressed in the failed proposals.\par \par \uc2 The latest rejection turns up the pressure on Deere management to resolve the dispute before customers start to defect to other equipment brands as orders and production gets under way for next year's models.\par \par \uc2 The proposal for a new six-year contract was voted down by a margin of 55% to 45%, the auto workers union said. It would have given more than 10,000 Deere workers on strike an immediate 10% pay raise and an $8,500 bonus for each worker if the deal had been ratified.\par \par \uc2 The company also offered 5% raises in 2023 and 2025. For the other three years of the contract, Deere employees would receive lump-sum bonuses amounting to 3% of their pay.\par \par \uc2 Union members rejected the first proposal Oct. 10, saying the raises and other improvements to benefits offered then were inadequate when Deere's farm and construction equipment sales are rising and other employers are boosting pay significantly to attract workers. Union workers at the Moline, Ill., company walked out on Oct. 14 at 14 plants, Deere's first strike in 35 years.\par \par \uc2 U.S. union officials have said more workers are looking to organize and push employers for better terms as companies grapple with a shortage of workers and broader supply-chain challenges. The Deere strike followed recent walkouts at snack company \uc2 Mondelez International Inc\uc2 ., commercial-truck maker Volvo and breakfast-cereal maker Kellogg Co. Baristas at some \uc2 Starbucks Corp\uc2 . stores and warehouse workers for \uc2 Amazon.com Inc\uc2 . have sought this year to organize, with mixed results.\par \par \uc2 Labor organizers have said that hours, wages and health concerns have mounted for workers as the Covid-19 pandemic has upended workplaces over the past year and a half. Workers have said they want employers to provide better pay, broader benefits and safer workplaces.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020211103ehb300020\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
89
