\par\par \b \uc2 Worker Shortage Could Bring Back Stagflation\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ron Johnson \par \uc2 795 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 November 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A17\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 I helped start an Oshkosh, Wis.-based plastics manufacturing business. It was 1979, in the midst of stagflation. Because inflation was endemic, we didn't think much about it. Like everyone else, we simply dealt with it. Price increases were expected and accepted throughout the economy.\par \par \uc2 Eventually President Reagan and Federal Reserve Chairman Paul Volcker broke the inflationary cycle. Prices stabilized, and for the remainder of my 30 years in manufacturing, getting customers to agree to a price increase was like pulling teeth. Every hike had to be fully justified and robustly negotiated. Now I'm hearing from businesses across Wisconsin and the U.S. that inflationary expectations have returned, and price and wage increases are becoming routine.\par \par \uc2 In the early 1980s, finding good workers was relatively easy. In Wisconsin, many young adults were leaving small family farms as the dairy business made the transition to larger operations with thousands of cows. A factory owner couldn't be blessed with better workers. They had a strong work ethic, initiative, and skill in fixing things. Those days -- those workers -- are long gone.\par \par \uc2 Even in the '80s, when good workers were plentiful, I worried that society's steady drumbeat that everyone had to get a college degree to be prepared for a high-tech future was seriously flawed.\par \par \uc2 For one thing, what did that say to kids who lacked the desire or aptitude for college? That they were destined to be second-class citizens? That the work that would be available to them was less valuable?\par \par \uc2 For another, if everyone went to college to become a manager or high-skilled tech worker, who would be left to build and make things? Who would work in fields and factories doing the physical and manual labor from which college promised to free everyone?\par \par \uc2 With virtually every business that employs such workers unable to fill positions, and the U.S. labor-force participation rate stuck at a 61.6% -- 5.7 points below its April 2000 high of 67.3% -- it would appear my concerns have been realized.\par \par \uc2 Because of staffing shortages, restaurants in resort areas in northern Wisconsin, in an effort to ensure food service for visitors, are cooperatively agreeing when each will be open and closed. Already severe worker shortages in healthcare, transportation and other areas of the economy are being exacerbated by resistance to vaccine mandates. In a roundtable discussion with nurses, I heard preventable deaths are occurring in emergency rooms because of staff shortages.\par \par \uc2 Imported products languish offshore, waiting to be unloaded because there aren't enough longshoremen and truck drivers. A shortage of microchips limits the production of automobiles and many other products. Manufacturers have to shut down shifts and limit production because of worker shortages and supply-chain dislocations. Consumer and producer price index increases are hitting levels not seen in decades.\par \par \uc2 I am describing the key ingredients for a new round of stagflation. As alarming as the prospect of prolonged higher inflation is, I am most concerned about a long-term shortage of workers throughout the economy. It is a shortage caused by the disparagement of blue-collar work, low birthrates, the retirement of baby boomers, a growing welfare state that pays and thereby encourages people not to work, and a broken immigration system.\par \par \uc2 Most political rhetoric regarding employment continues to focus on "creating" well-paying jobs. But when 10 million jobs remain unfilled and 51% of small-business owners say they can't fill open positions, public policy needs to focus on expanding the labor force and filling job openings.\par \par \uc2 As the \uc2 Congressional Budget Office\uc2  pointed out in projecting 1.8% average potential real growth between 2021 and 2031, there are two components to economic growth: productivity gains and increases in the labor supply. The CBO's 1.83% projection comprised 1.48% annual productivity gains and 0.35% annual growth in the potential labor force. Expanding the potential labor force 0.35% requires 588,400 additional workers a year on average.\par \par \uc2 But 1.83% annual growth is meager. Using this simplistic growth model, achieving 3% annual growth would require labor force growth of 1.5% or 2.6 million workers annually. With baby boomers retiring and Democrats creating more programs that will make it easier for people not to work, where will we get these workers?\par \par \uc2 My suggestion would be to stop creating new entitlements, fix the entitlements we have, reduce deficit spending, stop adding to our national debt, secure our border and fix our legal immigration system to allow enough workers in to satisfy labor growth without depressing wages. Unfortunately, these are not the policy proposals of the current administration.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Mr. Johnson, a Republican, is a U.S. senator from Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020211102ehb200012\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
