\par\par \b \uc2 Business News: Companies Offer Perks to Attract Workers\b0\par\par\uc2 By Nina Trentmann and Mark Maurer \par \uc2 835 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 October 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B6\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Finance chiefs are boosting salaries and recruitment spending, offering more in the way of perks and expanding their equity plans as they joust to attract -- and retain -- workers.\par \par \uc2 Employees have quit their jobs in droves this year as the economy has picked up after last year's pandemic slump. Workers handed in a seasonally adjusted 4.3 million resignations in August, a record since tracking began in 2000 that came after months of elevated departures, according to the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2 . Jobless claims last week dropped to the lowest level since March 2020.\par \par \uc2 The "Great Resignation" is exacerbating skills shortages across industries and forcing companies to pay more, driving up costs at a time of already high inflation. In a survey released last week, chief financial officers at U.S. businesses said quality and availability of labor was their No. 1 concern, with three-quarters of them stating they have difficulty hiring, according to Duke University's Fuqua School of Business, which conducted the poll with the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  Banks of Atlanta and Richmond.\par \par \uc2 Companies plan to keep hiring new workers and increasing non-wage compensation -- for example, for healthcare and other benefits, the survey of 301 CFOs found. Wage bills are forecast to rise by 6.9% this year and next, while wages for new hires are set to rise by about 10%, according to the survey.\par \par \uc2 Discount retailer \uc2 Big Lots Inc\uc2 ., furniture retailer MillerKnoll Inc., food distributor \uc2 Sysco Corp\uc2 . and software firm \uc2 Autodesk Inc\uc2 ., among others -- are wrestling with the situation.\par \par \uc2 Columbus, Ohio-based \uc2 Big Lots\uc2  has increased wages in certain locations, offered heftier employee discounts and doubled its referral bonuses to $500. In September, the company upped its hourly rate for workers by $3 to $18.50 at its Tremont, Pa., distribution center, in part due to intense competition for warehousing staff in the area, CFO Jonathan Ramsden said. He is also evaluating wages in other locations.\par \par \uc2 "We need to be tracking the number of stores that are understaffed," he said, adding that this is a "week-by-week, almost day-by-day exercise." \uc2 Big Lots\uc2  declined to state how much it spends on retention and recruitment. The company's average rate for hourly workers is about $14, a "low-to-mid" single-digit percentage increase from a year ago, Mr. Ramsden said. \uc2 Big Lots\uc2  has about 35,000 employees and plans to increase its head count by 3% to 4% next year as it opens new stores, he said.\par \par \uc2 MillerKnoll, the Zeeland, Mich.-based manufacturer of office chairs and other furnishings, has increased wages for most of its factory workers in North America over the past few months, CFO \uc2 Jeffrey Stutz\uc2  said. The company, formed this year when \uc2 Herman Miller Inc\uc2 . bought design firm \uc2 Knoll Inc\uc2 . for $1.8 billion, also raised salaries for employees in non-production roles.\par \par \uc2 Labor costs during the quarter ended Aug. 28 went up by $5 million compared with the prior-year period due to higher wages and overtime pay, Mr. Stutz said, adding that the company expects to spend less on overtime once current conditions ease.\par \par \uc2 Recent salary increases, however, will likely remain in place, according to \uc2 John Graham\uc2 , founder of the CFO survey and a professor of finance at the Fuqua School of Business.\par \par \uc2 "We expect these salary increases to be permanent," Mr. Graham said. "And they absolutely increase costs for the firms, putting pressure on the firm to increase prices of their own products and thus increasing inflation." In recent months, MillerKnoll has raised prices to offset higher costs, including for labor.\par \par \uc2 Salary growth at companies in the S&P 500 has been flat in recent years, with median compensation per employee totaling $70,496 in 2020, up from $68,410 in 2017, according to MyLogIQ, a data provider.\par \par \uc2 Houston-based \uc2 Sysco\uc2 , which serves businesses including restaurants, hotels and hospitals, spent $36 million on recruiting, training and retention during the quarter ended July 3. It expects those costs will remain elevated through at least the end of 2021, CFO \uc2 Aaron Alt\uc2  said. The company declined to comment on its past spending on those efforts.\par \par \uc2 In addition to bonuses, \uc2 Sysco\uc2  plans to launch a driver-training program to help new hires and other employees get a commercial driver's license.\par \par \uc2 "We're taking active steps . . . so that what should be transitory does not become permanent," Mr. Alt said. \uc2 Sysco\uc2  had about 58,000 employees as of July 3, up 1.8% from a year earlier, according to a filing.\par \par \uc2 The worker shortage also complicates matters for CFOs looking to fill positions in their finance departments. \uc2 Autodesk\uc2 , the San Rafael, Calif.-based provider of software applications, has over 1,000 open positions, including two roles for vice president of finance.\par \par \uc2 "It feels like the balance of power has changed from the recruiter to the recruit," CFO \uc2 Debbie Clifford\uc2  said. "I have never seen a market like this in my career."\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw509\pich480\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal7635\pichgoal7200
