\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Daycare Shortage Pinches Households --- Workforce participation takes a hit as millions of parents are having to scramble schedules\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kathryn Dill \par \uc2 624 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 October 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A6\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Children across the U.S. have returned to school in person and many daycare centers have reopened with vaccinated staffers. Still, a shortage of child-care workers is sending ripples across the U.S. workforce, stymieing employers as they try to hire more workers and sidelining the careers of women who would otherwise opt to remain in the labor force.\par \par \uc2 Household survey data released last week the \uc2 U.S. Census Bureau\uc2  shows more than seven million adults had to make adjustments in the previous four weeks when children under age 5 were unable to attend daycare due to safety concerns, consistent with recent months.\par \par \uc2 Experts say the shaky child-care situation is contributing to the lowest labor-force participation rates among women in the U.S. since the 1970s. In September, more than 300,000 women ages 20 and over dropped out of the workforce, according to an analysis of government data by the \uc2 National Women's Law Center\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "The pandemic worsened the realities of child care in this country," said \uc2 Hannah Matthews\uc2 , deputy executive director for policy at the Center for Law and Social Policy. Some programs closed permanently, while others reopened with new, more costly health and safety processes, including smaller group sizes, lower staff ratios and social distancing, she said.\par \par \uc2 Many daycare centers that shut repeatedly during the pandemic due to staff or child illnesses lost so much revenue from tuition that they closed permanently, Ms. Matthews said. Other centers are turning away families because they are short on staffing. Nearly 110,000 child-care workers left the labor force between February 2020 and September.\par \par \uc2 More than 100,000 child-care providers experienced closures early in the pandemic, according to a 2020 survey by the National \uc2 Association for the Education of Young Children\uc2 . Since then, one in 10 child-care jobs hasn't returned. A recent survey by \uc2 NAEYC\uc2  found that 80% of child-care centers surveyed were experiencing staffing shortages, and half of affected centers are serving fewer children.\par \par \uc2 Nearly a third of parents with safety-related daycare interruptions used vacation, sick days or other paid leave to care for their kids, according to Census Bureau data. Roughly a quarter said they used unpaid leave, while a similar percentage curtailed their work hours to care for children. Almost two million adults supervised one child or more while working. In each group, more women made adjustments in response to daycare disruptions than men.\par \par \uc2 Some parents have stopped working or switched to less demanding roles.\par \par \uc2 After giving birth to her first child in September 2020, Louise Manfredi returned to her job as an assistant professor at Syracuse University in January. She had been awarded a semester of research leave, allowing her control over her schedule, and her husband worked West Coast hours for a biotech company, so the couple traded off caring for their infant daughter during workdayswhile the other worked.\par \par \uc2 The arrangement was effective but exhausting, with Ms. Manfredi working mornings, nights and weekends.\par \par \uc2 She was set to resume teaching this fall but hasn't found steady child care. Hearing nothing from local daycares whose wait lists they had joined before their daughter's birth, the couple began interviewing nannies, initiating conversations with what Ms. Manfredi estimates as more than 50 potential providers. One woman worked for the family for three days before deciding she didn't want to live in Syracuse, while other candidates objected to the couple's preference for a fully vaccinated caregiver.\par \par \uc2 She opted to take unpaid leave from her tenure-track job for the fall semester.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Lauren Weber contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020211025ehap0001e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
