\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Baldwin Film Set Called Rife With Complaints of Workers\b0\par\par\uc2 By Dan Frosch, Katherine Sayre, Ben Kesling and Joe Flint \par \uc2 1,144 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 October 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The movie set on which Alec Baldwin discharged a prop firearm in an incident that killed one person and wounded another was hampered by worker complaints over pay and conditions, according to people familiar with the filming.\par \par \uc2 The camera crew walked off the set earlier this week, according to the people. Workers complained about pay, long hours, safety and housing options, some of the people said.\par \par \uc2 It couldn't be determined if the concerns contributed to the shooting incident Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Investigators on Friday were trying to assess whether a live round was fired by Mr. Baldwin with the prop gun -- which would be an anomaly on a movie set, say film prop experts -- or if the discharge of the prop firearm involved a blank.\par \par \uc2 "We're looking into every aspect of what type of projectile was in that firearm," said Juan Rios, a spokesman for the Santa Fe County Sheriff's Office.\par \par \uc2 The prop gun used in the incident discharged a projectile during the filming of a scene in the movie "Rust" near Santa Fe on Thursday, according to the sheriff's office. Halyna Hutchins, the 42-year-old cinematographer on the film, died in the shooting, the sheriff's office said. The film's director, Joel Souza, 48, was injured.\par \par \uc2 The producing company for the film, Rust Movie Productions LLC, in a statement called safety of cast and crew its top priority. "Though we were not made aware of any official complaints concerning weapon or prop safety on set, we will be conducting an internal review of our procedures while production is shut down. We will continue to cooperate with the Santa Fe authorities in their investigation," it said.\par \par \uc2 A representative for the production company didn't respond to a request for comment on working conditions or the walkout.\par \par \uc2 A call sheet for the film, reviewed by The Wall Street Journal, said the person in charge of the prop firearms on set that day -- known as an armorer -- was Hannah Gutierrez Reed. A search warrant issued to law officers on Friday identified the armorer on set as Hannah Gutierrez.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Reed didn't respond to a request for comment.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Reed, 24, had recently completed her first film as head armorer on "The Old Way" starring Nicolas Cage, according to a podcast interview she gave last month.\par \par \uc2 "I was really nervous about it at first, and I almost didn't take the job because I wasn't sure if I was ready, but doing it, it went really smoothly," Ms. Reed said of the Nicolas Cage film in the "Voices of the West" podcast interview.\par \par \uc2 She said in the podcast that her father Thell Reed, a veteran Hollywood armorer and movie firearms consultant, had trained her from a young age.\par \par \uc2 When reached on a cellphone of Mr. Reed, a man who answered said he didn't know about the accident and wasn't there.\par \par \uc2 Professionals on movie sets are trained to follow heightened safety protocols around weapons, said Stephen Lighthill, president of the American Society of Cinematographers.\par \par \uc2 "What it means is that somebody was tired, somebody didn't follow protocol, someone didn't hire the right person," said Mr. Lighthill, speaking generally.\par \par \uc2 Particularly on independent and lower budget films, such as "Rust," it isn't uncommon for there to be labor unrest or for production crews to be expected to work long hours with few breaks, say film production workers.\par \par \uc2 The Covid-19 pandemic has put even more pressure on film crews. Many have shrunk in size as part of the effort to curb the potential spread of the virus, according to production workers. Earlier this month, the production workers union, IATSE, reached a new, tentative contract agreement with studios and streaming services for better pay and healthier working conditions.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Rios of the sheriff's office said investigators had obtained search warrants to scour the movie set Friday. He said the armorer on the film set and others had been interviewed by investigators. Mr. Rios declined to release more details on the type of projectile discharged from the prop gun or what type of firearm was being used.\par \par \uc2 There haven't been arrests or charges in connection with the incident, he said.\par \par \uc2 Just before 2 p.m. local time on Thursday a fire-department dispatcher reported that two people "have been shot accidentally" on the film set, the Bonanza Creek Ranch studios, according to archived dispatch recordings. Minutes later, a first responder said, "Have one aircraft available and they won't be able to take two patients. I'll be reaching out to another one."\par \par \uc2 Ms. Hutchins was flown by helicopter to \uc2 University of New Mexico Hospital\uc2  in Albuquerque. Mr. Souza was taken to a local Santa Fe hospital for his injuries, according to the Santa Fe County Sheriff's statement. Mr. Souza was subsequently released from the hospital, said a person familiar with the situation.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Baldwin said on his \uc2 Twitter\uc2  feed Friday: "There are no words to convey my shock and sadness regarding the tragic accident that took the life of Halyna Hutchins, a wife, mother and deeply admired colleague of ours."\par \par \uc2 Mr. Baldwin said he was fully cooperating with the police investigation.\par \par \uc2 Joe Swanson, whose company, Motion Picture Blanks, has manufactured movie ammunition for about 35 years, says he supplied the "Rust" production with blanks and dummy cartridges. Mr. Swanson says Motion Picture Blanks makes roughly one million rounds a year, and has supplied productions including "Band of Brothers" and "Black Hawk Down."\par \par \uc2 Accidental fatal gunshots on Hollywood sets are rare but have occurred before. Brandon Lee, the son of Bruce and Linda Lee, was killed in 1993 by an accidental gunshot wound on "The Crow" set.\par \par \uc2 At least 43 people have died on film sets in the U.S. since 1990, according to an Associated Press investigation from 2016, while more than 150 suffered life-altering injuries.\par \par \uc2 "Rust" is a low-budget independent movie on which Mr. Baldwin was also a producer. Other stars include Frances Fisher and Travis Fimmel. In the movie, Mr. Baldwin plays a grandfather who tries to rescue his estranged grandson from being executed for an accidental murder.\par \par \uc2 The budget for the movie was under $6 million, a person familiar with the project said.\par \par \uc2 Creative Artists Agency\uc2  presold domestic distribution to The Avenue, a unit of distribution company Highland Film Group. The domestic rights were sold for about $2 million, the person familiar with that matter said.\par \par \uc2 BondIt Media Capital, a media financing firm, handled the funding for the movie.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Baldwin's acting career includes movies, TV shows, and commercials. He portrayed former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  on "Saturday Night Live."\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Ellen Gamerman, John Jurgensen and Allison Prang contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020211023ehan0002m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
