\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Municipal Workers Face Mandate --- Unions say many city employees will quit after New York mayor requires vaccination\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jimmy Vielkind, Christine Mai-Duc and Jon Kamp \par \uc2 656 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 October 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A6\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 New York City Mayor \uc2 Bill de Blasio\uc2  announced an order mandating that all municipal workers must get vaccinated against Covid-19, setting up a conflict with unions that said they opposed the new rule.\par \par \uc2 Mr. de Blasio\uc2 's move on Wednesday ended an existing option of weekly testing and means some 46,000 city workers need to get their first shot by Oct. 29 or potentially lose their jobs, a spokeswoman for the Democrat said. The order covers 160,500 workers, of whom about 71% are partially or fully vaccinated, according to official estimates. The city said 70% of police and 60% of fire department personnel have received at least one shot.\par \par \uc2 Leaders of several unions representing affected New York City workers said they expected hundreds or thousands of employees would leave their jobs rather than get vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 "You'll have police officers walking off -- firefighters, EMTs and paramedics walking off. Who's going to respond to these calls?" said Oren Barzilay, head of a union that represents paramedics and emergency medical technicians employed by the fire department. "We are in for dark days in our city, and it's crazy."\par \par \uc2 Mr. Barzilay said about half of his union's 4,100 members are unvaccinated.\par \par \uc2 Noncompliant employees will be placed on unpaid leave and City Hall officials plan to negotiate in the coming days with labor unions about procedures that would follow. The city already requires vaccines for educational and public healthcare workers. Among the 167,000 employees of the city's school system, 96% have complied.\par \par \uc2 A spokesman for the Police Benevolent Association of the City of New York said it would "proceed with legal action to protect our members' rights." During a news conference, \uc2 Mr. de Blasio\uc2  said he was confident the city's mandate would withstand any legal challenge, and that officials would use overtime and redeploy workers to continue services.\par \par \uc2 New York City's roughly 8,000 correction officers will face a later deadline of Dec. 1. The city has been struggling to maintain staffing levels at its Rikers Island jail complex.\par \par \uc2 The deadlines in some other states and cities that require their employees to get vaccinated, without a testing option, have arrived in recent days. Governments in those places are now taking steps to deal with thousands of employees who haven't complied. Some have quit or been dismissed, some have been placed on leave, and some have been given monthslong extensions to get their shots. Many of the unvaccinated work in law enforcement, firefighting and correctional institutions.\par \par \uc2 The question of whether to fire such employees is particularly challenging as many governments, like private businesses, are already struggling to recruit and retain workers.\par \par \uc2 Exemptions to the mandates are typically allowed for medical or religious reasons.\par \par \uc2 In Chicago, Superintendent of Police David Brown said Tuesday that 67% of the department's employees had reported whether or not they were vaccinated by the city's Oct. 15 deadline, compared with 79% of workers citywide. The deadline for Chicago employees to get vaccinated is late December.\par \par \uc2 City administrators were holding one-on-one meetings with workers who hadn't complied, Mr. Brown said. So far, they have met with hundreds of officers and civilian employees, and 21 have been placed on unpaid leave.\par \par \uc2 In San Francisco, Mayor London Breed, a Democrat, said Tuesday that 96% of 35,000 municipal workers are vaccinated and the city is conducting hearings in the cases of 396 who haven't complied with the city's mandate.\par \par \uc2 Los Angeles had required that its 53,168 municipal employees be vaccinated by Wednesday or seek an exemption. According to a proposal released Tuesday, city officials are considering extending the deadline to Dec. 18.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Kris Maher and Zusha Elinson contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020211021ehal00015\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
