\par\par \uc2 Technology\par \b \uc2 Protesters Gather at Netflix Office --- Los Angeles rally and employee walkout are spurred by anger at Chappelle special\b0\par\par\uc2 By Joe Flint \par \uc2 496 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 October 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B6\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 A group of protesters gathered outside one of \uc2 Netflix Inc\uc2 .'s Los Angeles offices Wednesday to express anger over the streaming giant's Dave Chappelle comedy special, as some employees released demands for management and staged a walkout.\par \par \uc2 In the special, "The Closer," Mr. Chappelle made jokes and remarks regarding gender that many in the transgender community, including some \uc2 Netflix\uc2  staffers, found offensive. The company has defended the special, citing reasons including the company's commitment to artistic freedom.\par \par \uc2 Carrying signs that read "Transphobia Isn't Funny" and "Trans Lives Matter," protesters demanded that \uc2 Netflix\uc2  offer more inclusive programming and be more sensitive to the effect its content can have on certain communities.\par \par \uc2 Some \uc2 Netflix\uc2  employees attended the event, but didn't speak. Supporters of the comedian also showed up and tried to disrupt the proceedings.\par \par \uc2 The event was organized by Ashlee Preston, a trans activist. "If your satire is punching down, you are being a bully," Ashlee Preston said. "I'm tired of companies like \uc2 Netflix\uc2  making money off of trauma to my community."\par \par \uc2 The extent of the walkout wasn't clear. Many employees are working remotely.\par \par \uc2 In a list of demands sent to \uc2 Netflix\uc2  management, a group of transgender employees called on the company to create a new fund to develop trans and nonbinary talent, recruit more trans people into leadership roles, and add a disclaimer "before transphobic titles that specifically flag transphobic language, misogyny, homophobia, hate speech, etc. as required."\par \par \uc2 The employees wrote that those changes and others were necessary "to avoid future instances of platforming transphobia and hate speech, and to account for the harm we have caused and will continue to cause until the below measures are put in place."\par \par \uc2 Netflix\uc2  said in a statement: "We value our trans colleagues and allies, and understand the deep hurt that's been caused. We respect the decision of any employee who chooses to walk out, and recognize we have much more work to do both within \uc2 Netflix\uc2  and in our content."\par \par \uc2 A Netflix spokeswoman declined to comment on the specific demands.\par \par \uc2 In an interview Tuesday, Netflix co-chief executive \uc2 Ted Sarandos\uc2  said he "screwed up" in his communications with staff over the controversy.\par \par \uc2 Also in attendance at Wednesday's protest was B. Pagels-Minor, who doesn't identify as male or female and uses they as a pronoun.\par \par \uc2 B. Pagels-Minor said they were fired by \uc2 Netflix\uc2  last week for disclosing sensitive information outside the company that ended up appearing in a Bloomberg story. In an interview, B. Pagels-Minor acknowledged sharing documents internally but denied providing the information to media outlets. "I categorically deny leaking information," B. Pagels-Minor said. "\uc2 Netflix\uc2  was my family, I have no desire to hurt them."\par \par \uc2 Netflix\uc2  said in the statement, "This employee admitted sharing confidential information externally from their \uc2 Netflix\uc2  email on several occasions."\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020211021ehal0001f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
