\par\par \b \uc2 Deere Workers Strike as Walkouts Spread\b0\par\par\uc2 By Bob Tita and Allison Prang \par \uc2 775 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 October 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Deere & Co\uc2 . workers went on strike for the first time in 35 years, extending a series of labor disputes at major U.S. companies during a nationwide labor shortage.\par \par \uc2 Work stopped at 12:01 a.m. Thursday at 14 U.S. plants for the farm- and construction-equipment giant, as 10,100 U.S. production workers represented by the \uc2 United Auto Workers union\uc2  walked off the job. Deere and union negotiators continue to have discussions and trade ideas, but the next face-to-face bargaining session is not expected until early next week, a person familiar with the matter said.\par \par \uc2 The Moline, Ill., manufacturer, known for its green and yellow tractors, faces worker demands for higher wages and broadened benefits as labor unions push similar measures at major U.S. food producers, restaurant chains and vehicle makers, in some cases launching strikes. For companies, the labor disputes come on top of escalating supply-chain costs and logistical challenges that have slowed production, and increased prices for consumers and other buyers.\par \par \uc2 Deere workers on Sunday voted down a tentative deal that would have provided pay raises and lump-sum bonuses over six years and enhanced benefits for retirees. While talks continued this week, negotiators failed to reach another agreement by the union's Wednesday deadline to avoid a walkout.\par \par \uc2 "Our members at John Deere strike for the ability to earn a decent living, retire with dignity and establish fair work rules," said Chuck Browning, director and vice president of the United Auto Workers' agricultural-implement department.\par \par \uc2 "We will keep working day and night to understand our employees' priorities and resolve this strike, while also keeping our operations running for the benefit of all those we serve," Deere Vice President of Labor Relations Brad Morris said.\par \par \uc2 The striking workers make up more than one-third of Deere's 28,100 employees in the U.S. About 4,000 production workers at non-union plants or represented by other unions remain on the job, the company said. Deere has about 14,000 management and administrative employees.\par \par \uc2 While UAW-represented workers are off the job, the company's contingency plans focus on maintaining distribution of parts and other services to support farmers and contractors using Deere equipment in their farm fields or at construction sites, the person familiar with the matter said. This strike comes at a typically slower time of the year for Deere, as farmers concentrate on harvesting this year's crops before making decisions about purchasing equipment for next year.\par \par \uc2 Strikes in the heavy-equipment industry have become infrequent in the past decade. Deere, in particular, has had a long run of contract negotiations without a walkout.\par \par \uc2 Workers at Deere and other companies are now going on strike against a backdrop of widespread labor shortages that have helped push up wages for existing employees, while employers add sweeteners such as signing bonuses to recruit new staff. Throughout the pandemic, many workers have been asked or required to work longer hours as the labor force shrinks and record numbers of Americans quit their jobs, raising the risk of burnout and resignations among those who remain, executives have said.\par \par \uc2 Fourteen-hundred employees at cereal maker \uc2 Kellogg Co\uc2 . have been on strike amid negotiations over a labor contract with the \uc2 Bakery, Confectionery, Tobacco Workers and Grain Millers International Union\uc2 . Workers represented by the \uc2 UAW\uc2  at commercial truck maker Volvo went on strike off and on during the spring and summer as members voted down three contract proposals, before agreeing to a six-year contract in July.\par \par \uc2 Bakery workers for food company \uc2 Mondelez International Inc\uc2 . last month ended a weekslong strike after agreeing to a new four-year contract that included hourly wage increases and a higher company match for 401(k) contributions. In Buffalo, N.Y., \uc2 Starbucks Corp\uc2 . is pushing back against a unionization drive by employees at area stores who are seeking expanded wages, training and staffing.\par \par \uc2 The International Alliance of Theatrical Stage Employees, which represents over 150,000 behind-the-scenes workers, said it would strike on Monday unless a deal for a new contract is reached with producers.\par \par \uc2 Under terms of Deere's tentative deal with the \uc2 UAW\uc2 , workers would have received wage raises of 5% to 6% this year, based on their job duties; 3% raises in 2023 and 2025; and lump-sum bonuses in three other years, according to a summary from the union. The deal also would have restored cost-of-living wage adjustments that had been part of previous Deere contracts.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Lauren Weber and Allison Prang contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020211015ehaf00026\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
