\par\par \uc2 Heard on the Street\par \b \uc2 PC Makers Plan on a New Normal --- \uc2 Dell\uc2 , \uc2 HP\uc2  see hybrid work widening market for the devices and related hardware, but sustainability is still a big question\b0\par\par\uc2 By Dan Gallagher \par \uc2 492 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 October 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B10\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 [Financial Analysis and Commentary]\par \par \uc2 The pandemic-driven boom for the personal-computer business is over. The big question now is where the market settles.\par \par \uc2 Global PC sales averaged around 265 million units annually in the five-year period before the pandemic's onset in 2020, according to IDC. That number jumped to 302.6 million units in 2020, as the pandemic sent workers and companies rushing to gear up for working at home. The rush continued well into this year, which IDC expects to end with more than 340 million PC units shipped.\par \par \uc2 That would be the best year for the industry since 2012, but it also reflects a notable slowdown already under way. Third-quarter PC sales grew less than 4% year over year following five straight periods of double-digit gains, according to IDC's data. And the firm's full-year projection suggests a 4% decline in the fourth quarter is likely. Both IDC and rival \uc2 Gartner\uc2  point to supply issues caused by the chip production shortage as constraining some sales, though both also concede demand has slowed in some segments. \uc2 Gartner\uc2  says laptop and Chromebook shipments in the U.S. fell 10% year over year in the third quarter.\par \par \uc2 The key question now is whether PC sales in general fall to their pre-pandemic norm or stabilize at a new, higher level. A case for the latter is persuasive. The adoption of long-term, hybrid work arrangements that allow employees to bounce between the office and home frequently means a higher number of workers will need more than one computer. It also could mean a larger market for peripherals such as webcams and speakers as the long-term reality of frequent Zoom calls sinks in.\par \par \uc2 HP\uc2  and \uc2 Dell\uc2  are banking on it. At its Sept. 23 analyst meeting, \uc2 Dell\uc2  projected average annual growth of 2%-3% for its PC segment over the next five years, with Client Solutions segment President \uc2 Sam Burd\uc2  claiming that the "PC industry has reset to a higher level." \uc2 HP\uc2  seems likely to tout a similar message at its own analyst meeting next week; Chief Financial Officer Marie Myers told a Citi conference last month that the company has great confidence in the outlook for its PC business, noting that the addressable market has "really expanded."\par \par \uc2 Most industry analysts seem to agree. Both IDC and \uc2 Gartner\uc2  project PC shipments of greater than 340 million units in 2022, and IDC sees that persisting through 2025. TrendForce, which projects a 7% decline in laptop unit sales in 2022, added in its updated forecast issued Tuesday that the laptop market "will undergo some growth in the medium to long term, without falling back down to pre-pandemic levels."\par \par \uc2 At least working from home is clicking for some.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw262\pich392\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3930\pichgoal5880
