\par\par \b \uc2 Rate of Workers Returning to Office Hits Pandemic High\b0\par\par\uc2 By Peter Grant \par \uc2 953 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 October 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 A widely anticipated surge in employees returning to the office after Labor Day never materialized. But as Covid-19 infection rates fall again, workers are trickling back to the office at the highest rate since the pandemic began.\par \par \uc2 Office-building use has been slowly rising after a number of businesses required employees to return at least part of the week. In other cases, workers are returning voluntarily with summer vacations over and their children back in school.\par \par \uc2 The number of workers returning to traditional office space has been edging higher since the week of Labor Day, when an average of 31% of the workforce was back in the 10 major cities monitored by \uc2 Kastle Systems\uc2 . The average hit 35% during the week ended Oct. 1 and 36% during the week ended Oct. 8, a new high during the pandemic period, said the security company that tracks access-card swipes.\par \par \uc2 Office usage looks poised to push higher as more companies indicate they will be welcoming office workers back in the weeks ahead. \uc2 BlackRock Inc\uc2 ., \uc2 Whirlpool Corp\uc2 . and \uc2 Lions Gate Entertainment Corp\uc2 . are among the companies that set return dates for October and early November.\par \par \uc2 "There are things we can accomplish together in the office that we can't do remotely," Lions Gate Chief Executive \uc2 Jon Feltheimer\uc2  wrote in a message to employees, who are required to begin alternating between the office and working at home this month.\par \par \uc2 Those return figures are still modest compared with the lofty expectations of the spring, when rising vaccination rates led many companies to say that a majority of their employees would be back at their desks at least part of the time in the early fall. Manhattan employers projected that 62% of their workers would be in the office by September, according to a survey released in June by the Partnership for New York City, a business advocacy group.\par \par \uc2 The spread of the Delta variant changed that. Businesses were forced to shelve reopening plans. Many workers who had returned to the office went back to working remotely, and office use dipped.\par \par \uc2 Now, infection rates are falling again -- in part because many companies and local governments are mandating vaccinations -- gradually easing workers' concerns about working inside with colleagues.\par \par \uc2 The rise in office occupancy has been particularly pronounced in New York, where usage increased to a pandemic high of 30% during the week ended Friday, up from 21% at the beginning of September, Kastle said.\par \par \uc2 That rise reflects the city's high vaccination rate, reopening of schools and the large number of companies in the finance business, according to Mark Ein, Kastle's chairman.\par \par \uc2 Banks, private-equity firms, hedge funds and securities firms are among "the most adamant about getting people back," he said.\par \par \uc2 The New York offices of engineering firm \uc2 Thornton Tomasetti\uc2  are about one-third occupied these days, compared with below 20% in August, said \uc2 Michael Squarzini\uc2 , the firm's co-chief executive. "There's definitely been a gradual increase of people to the office post-Labor Day," he said. "We always knew it was going to be slow."\par \par \uc2 In other parts of the country, the Delta variant has kept return-to-office rates in check. For example, Austin, Houston, Dallas and other Texas cities have remained at the top of the list throughout most of the pandemic. But during September, the return rates in these cities flattened out in the high 40% range, a reflection of the relatively high infection rate in the state and hurricane season, Mr. Ein said.\par \par \uc2 The emptying out of U.S. office space for most of the pandemic has been painful for restaurants, stores and other small businesses in office districts throughout the country. Some of these have now begun to benefit from workers trickling back.\par \par \uc2 Business is back to over 60% of what it was before the pandemic at Oceana, a Midtown Manhattan restaurant that relies heavily on the office crowd with corporate accounts. "If you were to come here Wednesday or Thursday night, you would look at me like: 'What pandemic?'" managing partner Paul McLaughlin said.\par \par \uc2 But Nikita Shimunov, who owns First Class Barber Shop near Grand Central Terminal in Manhattan, said he has barely noticed any difference. Standing outside his shop at lunchtime on a weekday last week, he tilted his head toward the empty chairs inside.\par \par \uc2 "See: lunchtime. Nobody. Before the pandemic it never used to be like this," he said. "If it's going to stay like this another five-six months, we can't hold on."\par \par \uc2 Few expect a surge in office workers returning for the rest of 2021. Companies such as \uc2 Alphabet Inc\uc2 .'s \uc2 Google\uc2  and \uc2 Wells Fargo & Co\uc2 . have delayed office openings until the beginning of 2022. Other businesses will likely opt for early next year, especially as the holiday season approaches and more employees take time off.\par \par \uc2 Law firm \uc2 Akin Gump Strauss Hauer & Feld\uc2  was among the businesses to delay reopening plans. The firm's new policy: Rather than choose a date in the distant future, it recently told employees that it would simply give them three weeks' notice before asking them to return to the office.\par \par \uc2 "It was time to get out of the prediction business," said \uc2 Kim Koopersmith\uc2 , the firm's chairwoman.\par \par \uc2 Even after most companies decide it is safe to return to the office, chances are that office space won't be filled as it was before the pandemic. Some companies such as accounting and consulting firm \uc2 PricewaterhouseCoopers LLP\uc2  plan to let large numbers of employees keep working remotely indefinitely.\par \par \uc2 Others are embracing a hybrid model that allows workers to split their time between traditional offices and home.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020211013ehad0002c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
