\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Shot Mandate For NYC Schools Bars Thousands\b0\par\par\uc2 By Lee Hawkins \par \uc2 526 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 October 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A6\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Thousands of New York City school staff were barred from returning to work Monday for failing to comply with a vaccination mandate that took effect Friday afternoon.\par \par \uc2 About 95% of the 150,000 employees at the city's Department of Education have been vaccinated, including 96% of teachers and 99% of principals, Mayor \uc2 Bill de Blasio\uc2  said Monday.\par \par \uc2 Roughly 4,000 employees represented by the \uc2 United Federation of Teachers\uc2  were missing from schools as of Monday afternoon, including about 2,000 teachers, the union said. Classes resumed in person on Monday as scheduled after school officials spent the weekend identifying potential shortages and deploying substitute teachers, the union said.\par \par \uc2 The vaccination rate among staffers in some critical roles remained lower compared with that of teachers. About 84% of the school safety agents who provide security on campuses were vaccinated, leaving about 1,800 of the officers on unpaid leave from schools on Monday, according to union officials.\par \par \uc2 Mr. de Blasio\uc2  said Monday he was optimistic that more employees based in the city's 1,600 schools would get vaccinated in the coming days.\par \par \uc2 "Every adult in our schools is now vaccinated. That's going to be the rule going forward. That's the way to keep kids safe," \uc2 Mr. de Blasio\uc2  said, stressing that the issue has been upheld by several levels of courts.\par \par \uc2 The update follows weeks of uncertainty and concern among educators and their unions that schools would experience staff shortages and have a hard time deploying the city's supply of 9,000 vaccinated substitute teachers and 5,000 vaccinated substitute paraprofessionals to schools that need them.\par \par \uc2 Under terms of the mandate, all school employees needed to show proof by Friday afternoon that they received at least one dose of a Covid-19 vaccine to avoid being placed on unpaid leave. Employees who got vaccinated over the weekend and provided proof of vaccination status by Monday morning were allowed to work as usual and placed back on active status, according to the DOE.\par \par \uc2 New York City, the nation's largest school district, is one of the first in the country to require school staffers to be vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 United Federation of Teachers\uc2  President \uc2 Michael Mulgrew\uc2  said that the union was helped by a mandate extension, which gave teachers several extra days to get vaccinated. "Thankfully, we had that delay, which we called for," Mr. Mulgrew said. "We were happy to get it, because we are in much better shape today that we would have been last Tuesday."\par \par \uc2 The DOE said that about 18,000 shots have been administered to school employees since Sept. 24.\par \par \uc2 On Monday afternoon, the District Council 37 public employees union, which represents nearly 20,000 school support staff such as paraprofessionals, cafeteria workers and crossing guards, said that as of Sunday 93% had provided proof of at least one dose of the Covid-19 vaccine, up from 68% at the beginning of September. Employees who wish to apply for medical or religious exemptions will be allowed to do so by 5 p.m. Tuesday, the union said.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020211005eha50000x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
