\par\par \b \uc2 Women in the Workplace (A Special Report) --- There's Another Gender Pay Gap: Stock Options: Women often don't know what to ask for, experts say. And companies don't tell them.\b0\par\par\uc2 By J.J. McCorvey and Julia Carpenter \par \uc2 799 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 September 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par R7\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Brooke Harley considers herself lucky.\par \par \uc2 When she sat down to talk to the then-chief financial officer of Lululemon Athletica about a business-development position in 2009, she had already spent four years as a corporate attorney, and her duties had included developing stock-option plans for companies. So, when it came time to negotiate her pay, she decided to focus on equity.\par \par \uc2 "That would be the life-changing thing at the end of the day," Ms. Harley says.\par \par \uc2 When her future boss offered her 10,000 stock options, she asked for four times that, she says, and was awarded 20,000 options with a strike price of about $10 a share. A spokesperson for Lululemon says the company doesn't comment on individual employee matters; Ms. Harley's former boss, who no longer works at \uc2 Lululemon\uc2  , says he recalls her focus on equity but not the details.\par \par \uc2 Within five years, as \uc2 Lululemon\uc2  grew into an athletic- and leisure-apparel giant trading at more than $80 a share, Ms. Harley says she amassed roughly $1.5 million from her options.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Harley is now chief executive of ClassRebel, an e-learning company that offers classes focused on wealth-building skills. She says she remembers a former colleague at \uc2 Lululemon\uc2  nearly walking out the door just as the 90-day exercise period on her options was approaching.\par \par \uc2 "She had no idea," says Ms. Harley. "How do you not know you have options in the money?"\par \par \uc2 Simply "not knowing" is one of many factors that could be contributing to a gap in equity compensation and holdings between men and women in the corporate world, according to researchers. An analysis of General Social Survey data conducted for The Wall Street Journal by researchers at the Rutgers Institute for the Study of Employee Ownership and Profit Sharing found that 24% of male employees hold company stock or stock options, versus 17% of female employees. According to the report, the average value of company shares held by male employees in 2018 was $104,902. For women, it was $26,361.\par \par \uc2 Since 2002, the average value has increased significantly for men -- by nearly $30,000 -- but has fallen almost $20,000 for women, according to the report, which didn't provide a reason.\par \par \uc2 For women, the first step in changing the dynamic is to know what to ask for, and when to ask for it. "As you move up the corporate ladder, that's where pay becomes much more discretionary, and there are a lot more things other than pay -- privileges, bonuses, things that people don't think to ask for -- that could be on the table," says Cathy Tinsley, a professor of management at Georgetown University.\par \par \uc2 "Oftentimes men, through their social networks, hear about things that are possible to ask for," says Dr. Tinsley. Women, researchers say, should take a page from their male counterparts and talk to peers and colleagues about how they set themselves up for success. From those conversations, a woman can draft a list of things to ask for beyond salary.\par \par \uc2 Companies have to do their part, as well, to ensure the negotiation process is equitable and that compensation packages reward individuals fairly, says Nicole Mason, president and CEO of the \uc2 Institute for Women's Policy Research\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 "You can teach people how to negotiate and you can be a great negotiator, but if you don't have the full information, you never know what only the employer knows," says Zev Eigen, founder and chief data scientist at Syndio, an analytics platform that helps employers identify pay discrepancies. "The burden should be on the employer to get this right."\par \par \uc2 Publicly posting information such as salary brackets and benefit options for various positions would allow employees to research compensation earlier on, says Dr. Eigen.\par \par \uc2 "A company could say, 'For this role, the stock options are X. Everyone gets the same when they enter this role,' " Dr. Mason says. "That is fair and equitable, and it removes the opportunity for bias."\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Mr. McCorvey and Ms. Carpenter are Wall Street Journal reporters in New York. Email them at jj.mccorvey@wsj.com and julia.carpenter@wsj.com.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 17%\par \par \uc2 of female employees held company stock or options in 2018, vs. 24% of men\par \par \uc2 24%\par \par \uc2 of male employees held options\par \par \uc2 $26,361\par \par \uc2 The average value of employee shares held by women in 2018\par \par \uc2 $104,902\par \par \uc2 The average value of employee shares held by men\par \par \uc2 Sources: National Opinion Research Center at the \uc2 University of Chicago\uc2  ; Rutgers Institute for the Study of Employee Ownership and Profit Sharing\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020210927eh9r00006\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
