\par\par \uc2 PERSONAL JOURNAL\par \b \uc2 Workers With Health Issues Face a Dilemma --- For the medically vulnerable, and their loved ones, returns to the workplace present tough choices as the Delta variant surges\b0\par\par\uc2 By Te-Ping Chen \par \uc2 1,226 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 September 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A11\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 For Brooklyn teacher Kathryn Malara, this month feels like the most difficult time in the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 As the school year kicked off, signs of more normalcy were emerging with her classroom at full capacity. But Ms. Malara, a 33-year-old computer science teacher, was scared. She has a compromised immune system and feared that despite being vaccinated, she was still vulnerable to Covid-19.\par \par \uc2 She thought about not going back to work but scuttled the idea. "I have to go back, I need a job. I need health insurance, and I desperately miss those kids," she said, the day before returning to school.\par \par \uc2 Less than a week later, Ms. Malara tested positive for Covid-19. She believes she contracted the disease at school because several teachers in adjacent classrooms also got it. She was ultimately hospitalized for treatment and is isolating at home.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Malara is one of the millions of Americans who are immunocompromised. This group makes up 2.7% of the adult population and includes cancer patients, transplant recipients and people with autoimmune diseases. For such people and their families, returning to the workplace means assuming an additional burden of risk, one that feels particularly frightening amid the rise of the Delta variant and, in some cases, a loosening of safety precautions.\par \par \uc2 Now, millions of immunocompromised workers and their loved ones are wrestling with their employer's policies. Many immunocompromised people, even when vaccinated, aren't able to generate the same levels of Covid-19 antibodies as people with robust immune systems do. And although immunocompromised people can get tests from their doctor to help determine their level of antibodies against Covid-19, these tests alone don't determine a patient's total risk.\par \par \uc2 On Thursday, the director of the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention said workers whose jobs place them at high risk for the disease should receive a booster of \uc2 Pfizer\uc2 's vaccine.\par \par \uc2 A spokesperson for the New York City Department of Education, which oversees the city school system, said that the city increased testing requirements last week and will now be testing weekly instead of biweekly. The department also cited guidance from the \uc2 Centers for Disease Control and Prevention\uc2 , which has urged a return to in-person schooling.\par \par \uc2 Under the Americans with Disabilities Act, workers with disabilities are able to request that their employers provide alternate arrangements to keep them safe, such as remote work or separate workspaces. Still, not all roles can accommodate such requests. And people who fear bringing Covid-19 home to vulnerable family members don't fall under such protections, says Emily Litzinger, a partner with labor and employment law firm Fisher Phillips LLP.\par \par \uc2 A tally by Fisher Phillips found that there have been more than 450 legal disputes over remote work to date, accounting for roughly 16% of lawsuits related to Covid-19.\par \par \uc2 In Oklahoma City, Emily Blount, 30, knows that the risks aren't theoretical.\par \par \uc2 Her mother, an accountant with the \uc2 Oklahoma Department of Corrections\uc2 , had an autoimmune condition and during the first months of the pandemic worked remotely. Shortly after returning to the office last year, following several infections among co-workers, she, too, contracted Covid-19. She died in August, her daughter says.\par \par \uc2 "She was 62, just a few years away from retiring," says Ms. Blount, who teaches at a local school. "She had all the good parts of life in front of her."\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Oklahoma Department of Corrections\uc2  said that because of the technical nature of her job, Ms. Blount's mother occasionally returned to the office and that it mourned her passing.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Blount is immunocompromised herself, having recently finished chemotherapy for breast cancer. She gets frustrated by people unwilling to wear masks, even to protect vulnerable people like herself.\par \par \uc2 For many immunocompromised people, their vulnerability has never been higher, says Erin Carlson, director of graduate public health programs at the University of Texas at Arlington.\par \par \uc2 "Prior to the vaccine, most people were being very careful, and we still had our mask mandate," says Ms. Carlson, who takes medication for an autoimmune disease that suppresses her immune system. Now, Texas has banned mask mandates, so that Ms. Carlson, who teaches in-person classes of up to 100 students, isn't permitted to require her students to wear a mask.\par \par \uc2 "I feel I'm very, very handcuffed in what I can do to be able to protect myself," says Ms. Carlson, adding that in some classes as many as 40% of students might be unmasked.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Food and Drug Administration\uc2  recently approved booster shots for certain severely immunocompromised individuals. Others have struggled to get their shots in the first place.\par \par \uc2 Crystal Seymore, a 41-year-old in Tulsa, Okla., until recently worked at a large call center providing customer-service support. The role offered no sick days. Although she has suffered from a compromised immune system all her life, Ms. Seymore said she feared that the side effects from the vaccine would force her to miss work, something she couldn't afford to do. She also feared that she would lose her job, if she missed work.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Seymore got her first shot only earlier this month after finding another job at a juvenile detention center that permitted her time off for the shot.\par \par \uc2 She still feels vulnerable, given the uncertainty over her response to the shots and the high number of people in town who refuse to mask or get vaccinated. "It's all about their rights, their freedom," she says. "What about my freedom? I didn't ask to be born with this."\par \par \uc2 Some workers with immunocompromised family members, like Julie Conboy Russo, 60, in Levittown, N.Y., have wound up quitting their jobs to try to protect loved ones.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Conboy Russo had worked as a nurse for 38 years. She left her job earlier this year to try to protect her husband, a leukemia patient who receives regular chemotherapy treatments. She says she misses her patients but didn't want to jeopardize her husband's health.\par \par \uc2 "I couldn't do it anymore," says Ms. Conboy Russo, who is currently pursuing her Ph.D.\par \par \uc2 Other workers say they have felt no choice but to quit to protect themselves. This summer, finance project consultant Jessica Olson, 44, learned that her employer, Blue Cross Blue Shield of Illinois, a division of \uc2 Health Care Service Corporation\uc2 , was mandating a return to the office this month. With help from her physician, Ms. Olson filed a request for an accommodation under the Americans with Disabilities Act, asking for permission to stay remote.\par \par \uc2 The company denied her request. "I'm not asking you for the sun, moon and stars. I'm simply asking you to work from home during a pandemic," she says.\par \par \uc2 HCSC said it couldn't comment on individual employees but that it complies with the Americans with Disabilities Act. "We continue to prioritize the health and safety of our employees, while also meeting our commitments to customers, providers and other stakeholders," a spokesperson said, adding that HCSC has permitted employees to work remotely two days a week.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Olson quit and is looking for a new job.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020210927eh9r0000c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
