\par\par \b \uc2 Women in the Workplace (A Special Report) --- Redefining Ambition: A comprehensive new study by McKinsey and Lean In shows how burned out and restless many professional women are. And many of them are rethinking what they want from their careers.\b0\par\par\uc2 By Vanessa Fuhrmans and Lauren Weber \par \uc2 2,352 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 September 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par R1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Working more than a year under pandemic conditions threw into sharp relief what Vicki Klaker wanted from her career -- and wasn't getting.\par \par \uc2 At first, the marketing specialist and mother of five plowed through the long days of working from her home near Wichita, Kan., while overseeing her children's online learning.\par \par \uc2 Soon, though, "remote work was a double-edged sword," the 38-year-old says.\par \par \uc2 Being more available to her family was gratifying and made her wonder whether another line of work would let her spend more time with them. In her corporate job at a fast-growing restaurant chain, working from home meant monitoring emails often into the evening, leaving her both depleted and unfulfilled.\par \par \uc2 "I realized, 'I'm not being fulfilled because I'm not helping people, and I don't want to waste any more time,' " says Mrs. Klaker, who had always dreamed of becoming a teacher. This spring, she applied for a position at an area school district and, to her surprise, got it. As of last month, she teaches high-school students how to code and build websites, and isn't looking back. "My definition of success has changed," she says.\par \par \uc2 The pandemic has been especially brutal for women. They suffered a disproportionate number of layoffs, took on more home responsibilities and made more career sacrifices to manage the collapse of in-school learning and child-care arrangements, research shows.\par \par \uc2 But those stresses have also motivated many women to redefine their ambitions and reset the terms of their careers. Some, like Mrs. Klaker, are forging new careers to pursue old passions or switching to jobs better suited to family lives. Some are starting new businesses, while others have moved to far-flung places or taken a pause to plot their next career move. And many are taking the leap to bigger roles, but with greater expectations of work flexibility than in pre-Covid-19 times.\par \par \uc2 New, major research shows just how restless -- and spent -- many professional women are. Thousands of men and women surveyed this summer for the seventh annual Women in the Workplace study by \uc2 McKinsey & Co\uc2 . and LeanIn.Org reported burnout at even higher rates than last year -- by then, several months into the pandemic. But women appear to be feeling the exhaustion more acutely. About 42% said they felt burned out often or almost always, compared with 35% of men. Among female team managers, more than half described themselves as burned out, while 41% of their male peers did.\par \par \uc2 One-third of women said they had considered leaving or downshifting their careers -- either temporarily or altogether -- over the past year, while 27% of men said the same. And 40% of both men and women said they had contemplated switching to another employer.\par \par \uc2 The findings come from one of the most comprehensive pandemic-era surveys of working women and men, in which researchers at McKinsey and Lean In polled more than 65,000 North American employees. And they suggest that women -- as well as many men -- are having a collective moment of reflection over what they want from their work lives, says \uc2 Lareina Yee\uc2 , a senior partner at McKinsey.\par \par \uc2 A big reason, she says, is that women are still shouldering much of the unpaid work at home. Meanwhile, female managers, especially, have become linchpins in supporting other employees though the pandemic, the data shows. Across the board, people who reported to women were more likely to say their boss checked in on their well-being, helped them navigate work-life challenges and ensured their workloads were manageable. Yet more than a third of companies surveyed said that work goes largely unrecognized. "Women are asking, 'What's the return on investment of my job, what's the best use of my time?' " Ms. Yee says.\par \par \uc2 Stacy Brown-Philpot\uc2 , the former chief executive of TaskRabbit, sees the quest for change among women in her own network. "We had so many things taken away from us," she says of the way the pandemic stripped away social activities, restaurant meetups, even the safety of venturing out sans mask. With many women's lives reduced to work and home life, " 'What do I really want?' has become a real question," she says.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Brown-Philpot says her own moment of clarity arrived with the murder of \uc2 George Floyd\uc2  by a Minneapolis police officer. She was already planning to leave the online gig-worker marketplace, but was still plotting her next move. When protests swept the country, she didn't want to just talk about the need for societal change.\par \par \uc2 "How can I use the platform I have built as a leader in Silicon Valley, as a leader in technology, to inspire and invest in the next generation of leaders?" she recalls asking herself.\par \par \uc2 The answer -- conceived in a flurry of WhatsApp messages with SoftBank Group International CEO \uc2 Marcelo Claure\uc2  and Paul Judge, co-founder of Pindrop and Panoramic Ventures -- was the creation of SoftBank's $100 million Opportunity Fund. The fund, which supports companies led by people of color -- who are often shut out of venture-capital funding -- has invested over $50 million in more than 60 companies, says Ms. Brown-Philpot, who helps oversee the fund.\par \par \uc2 For many aspiring female entrepreneurs, the pandemic has brought urgency to dreams of launching a business that not long before seemed far off or out of reach. Roughly half of people who started businesses in 2020 were women, according to a May study by human-resources-technology provider Gusto, up from 27% in recent years.\par \par \uc2 Before the pandemic, 25-year-old Toronto landscape designer Anna Monaldi Crombie thought she would strike out on her own one day, maybe after completing a master's degree or paying her dues at other firms, even if the work didn't always align with her convictions about ecology and sustainability.\par \par \uc2 By spring 2020, though, demand for landscape design surged as locked-down homeowners sought to turn yards into places to socialize and to escape from the indoors. Ms. Crombie's job became chaotic and stressful amid staffing shortages. The pandemic also prompted reflections on death and mortality.\par \par \uc2 "I thought, 'This is my life, I'm going to die eventually. Do I want to spend it doing something I don't enjoy and work that I don't value?' " she says.\par \par \uc2 That summer, she gave notice at her firm and started Anna Monaldi Designs. Hanging out her shingle felt risky, but in her first year, her take-home pay increased by about 25%. More valuable, she says, is that she works fewer hours than before, giving her time for camping, long walks and enjoying the outdoors.\par \par \uc2 For other women, like Natalie Mazzie, the strains of the pandemic led to a more dramatic break. In early 2021, the 36-year-old was a program manager for the customer-experience team at a tech firm in Seattle.\par \par \uc2 "It was super fun and intense. It was also hard," she says. Five of Ms. Mazzie's family members and friends had died of Covid in 2020. "I tried to ignore that because I didn't want to lose my job. I felt lucky to have anything."\par \par \uc2 Eventually the stress and long hours, along with volunteer commitments, such as leading the company's resource group for Latino employees, caught up with her. She took a three-month leave to reflect and decided she needed a reset. "I was thinking, 'Why am I letting my job take over my entire life?' I was waking up feeling miserable, then going to sleep extraordinarily late because it was the only time I had to myself," she says.\par \par \uc2 She gave notice at her job in August, and in early September she used her savings to move to an olive farm in Greece for a month. Her room is on the top floor of a farmhouse with a view of the sea. Ms. Mazzie reads, meditates and does yoga.\par \par \uc2 She plans to stay in technology. But she's going to give priority to workplaces that appreciate employees' contributions and value career development, upskilling and employee well-being. "I want work-life balance," she says. "I know that now. I didn't really before."\par \par \uc2 By the end of last year, women hadn't lost ground in management ranks, though it remains to be seen whether the stress and soul-searching many have experienced during the pandemic will affect their representation this year and beyond.\par \par \uc2 Women made minor advances at nearly every level of the corporate ladder in 2020, the McKinsey and Lean In data shows. The biggest notch upward was in C-suites, where the share of female executives rose to 24% from 21% the prior year, according to the study, which examined workforce data from 423 companies with a collective 12 million employees.\par \par \uc2 To be sure, some women lost jobs and haven't re-entered the workforce, or found the demands of the pandemic to be too much and bowed out, at least temporarily. And the McKinsey and Lean In data captures the experience of mostly white-collar women. There were 1.2 million fewer mothers of children under age 18 in the labor force last month compared with August 2019, a decline of 4.6%, according to census data.\par \par \uc2 But the fact that more mothers didn't give up careers amid the demands of work and home life during the pandemic shows that "women are latching on to the labor market and hanging on with a lot more ferocity and stamina than they ever have in the past," says Misty Heggeness, an economist with the Census Bureau.\par \par \uc2 The numbers point to two permanent trends with women and work, she says: Few households can afford to lose the income women bring in, and "women these days don't see working outside the home as a secondary component of who they are."\par \par \uc2 This spring, Arquay Harris made the leap from her position as a senior director of engineering at Slack to a bigger role as vice president of engineering at software-development platform Webflow, where she now sets the culture and direction for an entire technical organization. But first, she asked herself whether she had the wherewithal to go for it.\par \par \uc2 "It wasn't a question of whether I can do this, but more, do I have the emotional cash flow, the will to want to do it?" she says.\par \par \uc2 Ultimately, her desire to have greater impact outweighed her fatigue from the pandemic and a season of racial and political divisions. "The pandemic didn't derail me, but it put things in focus," says Ms. Harris, 43. "If you think about your highest aspiration, that can help you chart your journey. Mine was not, 'I have to be VP or [chief technology officer].' It was more like, 'I like the ability to connect and to mentor.' "\par \par \uc2 Many women say greater flexibility will be key to accomplishing what they want to in their careers. The vast majority of employees who said their companies gave them the flexibility to balance their work and personal lives also reported being happy in their jobs, according to McKinsey and Lean In. Post-pandemic, two-thirds of women -- and 57% of men -- said they want to work remotely at least three days a week.\par \par \uc2 "The freedom of remote work has really leveled the playing field for me," says Jenica Nasworthy, a senior managing editor in \uc2 Simon & Schuster\uc2 's children's division. Now working from the Queens, N.Y., apartment she shares with her husband and two children, she no longer has to race out of the office to make a daycare or school pickup. She can slip out to get her daughter at preschool across the street.\par \par \uc2 "I really love what I do, it's part of why I threw myself into my work," says the 41-year-old. "The hard part was always the conflict between office hours and child-care hours." Her tenure and higher productivity, she says, has given her the confidence to ask for such flexibility when the company announces its office-reopening plans.\par \par \uc2 The challenge for companies? "How do we set up flexibility so that it actually doesn't create this sensation that you have to work all the time," says Rachel Thomas, CEO of Lean In, the nonprofit founded by \uc2 Facebook Inc\uc2 .'s \uc2 Sheryl Sandberg\uc2  to support women in their career ambitions. Ms. Thomas cautions that remote work may reduce the visibility of women who have struggled to get noticed in the workplace. Among employees surveyed by McKinsey and Lean In, white men were most likely to say they planned to be back in the office most of the week, Black women the least.\par \par \uc2 Now at work in a classroom instead of remotely from home, Mrs. Klaker says she is working harder than ever to get her new career off the ground. Leaving for school at 5:45 each morning, she teaches until 3 p.m., then grades papers or takes online training toward her teaching certification. On weekends, she tackles homework for a master's degree in education.\par \par \uc2 Yet she gets to spend many of those hours with her family. On a recent weekend, her eldest daughter helped her craft a lesson plan as they watched her son's baseball practice. And now that she and her husband, a band and choir teacher, have similar schedules, they plan a long family road trip to Michigan's Upper Peninsula next summer.\par \par \uc2 "It's going to be taxing for a while, but it's doing what I want to do," she says.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Ms. Fuhrmans is deputy chief of The Wall Street Journal's careers & workplace bureau, and Ms. Weber is a Journal reporter, both in New York. They can be reached at vanessa.fuhrmans@wsj.com and lauren.weber@wsj.com.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw640\pich243\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal9600\pichgoal3645
