\par\par \b \uc2 Women in the Workplace (A Special Report): Work & Life --- How to Balance Housework: The pandemic widened the chore gap for some couples. It's time to fix that.\b0\par\par\uc2 By Rachel Feintzeig \par \uc2 932 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 September 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par R8\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Afew years ago Sarah Cortell Vandersypen was feeling "so overwhelmed with everything." So, she decided to type it all up.\par \par \uc2 The fundraising professional and mom of one created a spreadsheet and entered every chore she could think of: taking out the trash, making her daughter's bottles, replacing empty paper-towel rolls. She marked who was doing what, color-coding as she went (green for "frequently," red for "rarely"), and created a separate column to denote how time-consuming the task was.\par \par \uc2 When the list was done, by her tally, she had 22 items marked green. Her husband had 14.\par \par \uc2 "It was validating," says the 34-year-old, who lives outside Baton Rouge, La.\par \par \uc2 Women have long carried more of the domestic load. For some couples, the pandemic -- with its endless dirty dishes and cluttered makeshift home offices and classrooms -- widened the gap. Both men and women ramped up the average time they spent on household activities in 2020, according to the American Time Use Survey, men by 16 minutes a day and women by 11.\par \par \uc2 But overall, women did far more -- 2.4 hours daily, compared with 1.6 for men. Meanwhile, the percentage of men doing housework and food prep each day decreased from 2019 to 2020, while the percentage of women doing those things increased, according to the survey, from the \uc2 U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "I did not see men doing it all," says Eve Rodsky, the author of "Fair Play," a book about dividing up housework more equitably. She has interviewed 75 couples via Zoom during the pandemic. The women are burned out, stressed and full of rage about unequal distribution of domestic labor, she says.\par \par \uc2 The men are finally starting to wake up to the problem. Many male partners have spent much of the past 18 months working from home, with a full view of labor that was formerly invisible.\par \par \uc2 Of course, some men have always taken on more housework than their female partners. Other people struggle with roommates, carry the weight of the household themselves as single parents or are in same-sex relationships where disparities don't have anything to do with gender.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Rodsky recommends households evaluate which obligations they need in their lives, and which feel superfluous. Figure out a joint "minimum standard of care" for the stuff that remains.\par \par \uc2 When you divide up chores, make sure one person is responsible for the entirety of the task -- so that the other person is completely freed from having to think about it.\par \par \uc2 After Kerry O'Keefe ramped up her work hours last November to contribute more to her family financially, her husband acknowledged she was still doing the brunt of the household tasks.\par \par \uc2 The couple was able to divvy up responsibilities more fairly, splitting school drop-offs and morning wakeups for their two children. He has continued checking in to see how she's feeling about everything on her plate. "It's like the most sexy thing he says to me," she says.\par \par \uc2 But she still feels the mental load of the household, weighing her down and distracting her. "There's so many details always running through my head," she says.\par \par \uc2 Nearly 80% of mothers said they were the one primarily responsible for family housework, according to an April 2020 survey analyzed by researchers at the \uc2 University of Pennsylvania\uc2 , \uc2 New York University\uc2  and the \uc2 University of Texas at Austin\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The researchers also found that when mothers were the only parent working remotely, 76% of them reported spending more time on housework. When only dad stayed home, 43% of men said the same.\par \par \uc2 Joshua Coleman, a San Francisco Bay Area psychologist and author of the book "The Lazy Husband: How to Get Men to Do More Parenting and Housework," says couples he works with often want, and start out having, egalitarian relationships. Then they have children, and it all falls apart.\par \par \uc2 Solving the problem begins with talking, he says. Don't broach the conversation when you're resentful. Start with a compliment. And don't frame the issue as a criticism. Instead, describe how it makes you feel when your partner leaves the pans in the sink overnight after promising to wash them.\par \par \uc2 When Felicia Gonzalez's fiance, Jason Gogel, moved into her Westborough, Mass., home earlier this year, he brought a lot of stuff, and a more lackadaisical approach to chores. The 36-year-old volunteered to take on laundry, but he wasn't into folding it. Baskets of wrinkled clothes languished around the house.\par \par \uc2 "I was falling into the 'Well, he didn't do it the way I'm used to'" trap, says Ms. Gonzalez, a single mom who had spent years establishing a rhythm around housework with her 8-year-old.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Gogel felt like Ms. Gonzalez's standards were too high. "If I can't do it right, I'm not going to do it," he says he sometimes found himself thinking.\par \par \uc2 Once Ms. Gonzalez explained why folding the clothes promptly was important to her, he stepped up. Together, they used the Marie Kondo method to declutter and divided chores by what they were good at and liked. Mr. Gogel, for one, finds vacuuming cathartic.\par \par \uc2 "Speak up," Ms. Gonzalez advises. "Ultimately, our partners are not mind-readers."\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Ms. Feintzeig writes The Wall Street Journal's Work & Life column. She can be reached at rachel.feintzeig@wsj.com.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020210927eh9r00007\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
