\par\par \b \uc2 C-Suite Strategies (A Special Report) --- How to Find a Family-Friendly Workplace: It's easy to say you want a better work-life balance. It's harder to know which companies will provide it.\b0\par\par\uc2 By Alex Janin \par \uc2 2,059 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 September 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par R1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 When Sarah Brown started her job search in February 2019, she had plenty to contend with, including changing her career from teaching to technical editing.\par \par \uc2 But the hardest part of the process was finding out if potential employers would offer what she wanted -- flexibility.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Brown, a mother of two small children, was looking for a job that would allow her to take last-minute trips to the doctor's office when her children got sick, and work varied hours to accommodate the children's schedules. But even with lots of searching, it was tough to find out which companies in her industry offered family-friendly benefits and flexible working hours.\par \par \uc2 "It's hard to find good information about how family friendly companies are until you get to the interview phase," says Ms. Brown, now 34. "I honestly could not discover much about any place that I researched just based on their online presence."\par \par \uc2 As workers reassess their priorities amid the pandemic, many are searching for family-friendly jobs. A recent survey of more than 2,000 workers by Prudential found that a quarter of workers plan to look for a new job after the Covid-19 pandemic, and 38% cite challenges with work-life balance as the reason.\par \par \uc2 But how can you job seekers find out how family friendly a company really is? A list of leave policies on a website often sheds little light on things like whether workers' workloads will prevent them from taking all that leave, and whether leaving early for an appointment will be frowned upon. Even a company that promotes some family-friendly policies, such as parental leave or a work-from-home option, may not have a culture that promotes flexibility and openness about child-care challenges and autonomy.\par \par \uc2 Here are the steps career experts and job seekers recommend people take when searching for a company that will offer the family-friendly benefits and flexibility that's right for them.\par \par \uc2 Name your needs\par \par \uc2 One person's definition of family friendly might be different from another person's. So each person should identify the policies and aspects of office culture that are most important to them, whether those are paid maternity and paternity leave, flexible working hours and more.\par \par \uc2 For Ms. Brown, having a flexible schedule was more valuable than a higher salary. She says she turned down a job that offered about $15,000 more than she was initially offered at her current job "because I really value the flexibility of my current company."\par \par \uc2 After about six months, she found an employer -- through a word-of-mouth recommendation -- that offered unlimited paid leave and mental-health days during Covid, among other things.\par \par \uc2 It is also important for job seekers to figure out what they're willing to give up to get flexibility -- and what they can't give up.\par \par \uc2 Mandy Cruz, a mother of six and currently a credit analyst at a bank in Merrillville, Ind., says she took a job at \uc2 H&R Block\uc2  after seeing it advertised on career websites as "great for working moms." The position offered a lot of flexibility for those looking to work part time, she says. However, the lack of paid time off, benefits and consistent year-round work made it difficult to support her family, she says.\par \par \uc2 "I needed something reliable, something that I could count on where I knew if I took the day off I wouldn't lose a day's pay," says Ms. Cruz, whose husband lost his job during the Covid-19 pandemic. The bank where she works now, she says, offers the option to work from home and pays a salary instead of an hourly wage, among other advantages.\par \par \uc2 A spokesperson from \uc2 H&R Block\uc2  says some of its seasonal associates have traditional 9-to-5 weekday schedules, others can set their own schedules, and some are eligible to work from home and enroll in medical benefits and a 401(k) plan.\par \par \uc2 Check what the company says online . . .\par \par \uc2 Even if companies don't spell out their policies on their own sites or social media, it's possible to figure out a lot by reading between the lines.\par \par \uc2 First, job seekers can follow the companies they are interested in on various platforms like \uc2 LinkedIn\uc2 , \uc2 Twitter\uc2  and \uc2 Instagram\uc2 , to get a sense of what they promote or don't. People can look for posts about events like "bring your kid to work day" or remote training that could indicate flexible, family-friendly environments.\par \par \uc2 Likewise, job seekers can look at the firms' own websites. Many companies provide career sites that can shed some light on their benefits, culture and priorities, says Romy Newman, president and co-founder of company-review site Fairygodboss.\par \par \uc2 Suzanne Brown, an author and work-life-balance consultant focusing on working parents, says, "Paternity leave is a good indicator that a company realizes it's not just about mothers."\par \par \uc2 Neha Khurram, a Toronto-based career coach and talent consultant, recommends searching for keywords like "compassionate" or "mental health" to get a read on whether the company promotes time off and emotional wellness. Conversely, repeated, "obsessive" mentions of words like "growth" and "ownership" could be indicators of a high work volume and less flexibility, she says.\par \par \uc2 . . . and what others say about it\par \par \uc2 Websites that offer reviews of workplaces can also provide a good picture of a company's policies and culture. Glassdoor, a site that invites current and former employees to leave anonymous ratings and reviews of their companies, has a "Family & Parenting" tab under the "Benefits" section of its company profiles. (But the site cautions that the listed information "may not be complete.")\par \par \uc2 Sites like Fairygodboss and InHerSight collect employee reviews focused on their companies' support of women. The Mom Project, a Chicago-based organization that helps place women in jobs, allows job seekers to filter available jobs by hours a week and remote or in-person work. A website called List Your Leave allows employees to leave reviews with details on maternity and paternity leave.\par \par \uc2 The idea is to look for patterns on these sites. What topics repeatedly come up under the company's "pros" and "cons" sections? And what topics don't come up? If flexible work is important to a job seeker, but "work from home," "WFH" or "remote" don't appear anywhere in the keywords, that could be a red flag.\par \par \uc2 There are also plenty of lists that focus on family-friendly benefits, such as Working Mother magazine's best-companies lists and Monster.com's list of companies with caregiver-support benefits, as well as industry-specific lists such as Yale Law Women's top law firms for gender equity and family friendliness. The National \uc2 Association of Women Lawyers\uc2  website offers annual reports on retention and promotion of women in various firms.\par \par \uc2 If a company appears on more than one list, it is probably doing something right, says Ms. Brown, the work-life balance consultant.\par \par \uc2 Look for clues during the interview\par \par \uc2 Job candidates can also keep an eye and ear out for hints that a company is -- or isn't -- family friendly while going through the interview process. For instance, is there an on-site child-care center or a fitness center? Do employees appear frazzled, disorganized and stressed out? Does the company do follow-up emails late at night?\par \par \uc2 If an interview is conducted remotely, it could indicate that a company is friendly to the idea of working from home. "A natural question might be, 'I see you're working from home. Is that something that the company promotes?'" says Amber Clayton, director of the HR Knowledge Center at the Society for Human Resource Management.\par \par \uc2 Find people in the know\par \par \uc2 Tapping into an alumni or other professional network may help job seekers connect, confidentially, with former employees at potential companies. "A lot of the times, people will be very open about why they left and what to look out for," says Ms. Khurram.\par \par \uc2 Experts say that job candidates should avoid reaching out to anyone currently employed by the company unless the hiring manager connects them with that person directly -- to avoid the risk that the inquiries could get back to the HR team or hiring manager. Some companies also provide interviewees the ability to shadow employees or speak to other members of the team to get a feel for the day-to-day culture.\par \par \uc2 Other possible sources of information: interviews, articles, podcasts or speeches by recently departed, high-profile executives or senior leaders. If a former leader implies that she left because she wanted more work and life balance, or because she felt like she wasn't getting enough time or support for her family, "that's when alarm bells should go off," says Ms. Brown, the work-life balance consultant.\par \par \uc2 Don't be afraid to ask questions\par \par \uc2 A lot of job candidates may be reluctant to ask about family-friendly policies early on. That makes sense for people who might be in a vulnerable financial position or have few options.\par \par \uc2 But others should be less wary. "A lot of times, employers will appreciate you asking them early on rather than later," says Ms. Khurram. Doing so can empower both the candidate and employer to discern early whether or not it is a good fit -- and nobody's time is wasted.\par \par \uc2 If job candidates are worried about working longer hours, they can ask about the team's size and get a sense of the projects. Experts say they should consider language like, "What are some of the team's major projects in the coming months, and is the team well positioned to complete them?" If the interviewer says they are still looking to hire many other team members, it might be a sign that other employees have to pick up the slack for the open roles.\par \par \uc2 During her first interview with her current firm, Ms. Brown, the mother of two, asked questions about mental-health days and incentives the company could offer to families with young children.\par \par \uc2 "They are interviewing me, but I'm also interviewing them because I think that workers have a lot more power than we think," says Ms. Brown.\par \par \uc2 The 2021 Yale Law Women report on top firms recommends that interviewees ask questions like, "Does the firm promote associates who have taken advantage of alternative work schedules?" and "How did your firm support employees and working parents during Covid-19?"\par \par \uc2 If all else fails, be the change\par \par \uc2 When Saira Rahman started looking for a new job during the pandemic, she knew she wanted to land at a company that would support her journey to becoming a parent. Online-banking platform HMBradley already had a good family-leave policy, she says, but didn't have fertility and surrogate-support programs.\par \par \uc2 "When I was negotiating my contract, I said, 'Hey, fertility is a really important thing to me,'" says Ms. Rahman, now vice president of finance at the Los Angeles-based company. "So I asked them to cover my egg freezing and then I asked them to cover it going forward for all of our employees."\par \par \uc2 It worked. HMBradley now covers up to $20,000 for fertility support, from egg freezing and sperm freezing to in-vitro fertilization and surrogacy. "Ultimately, we try to build our people policies around the concept of, 'If you treat your employees well, they will treat you well,'" HMBradley co-founder and CEO Zach Bruhnke said in an email to The Wall Street Journal.\par \par \uc2 Some firms are making their policies more flexible in light of the labor shortage fueled by the pandemic, says Ms. Clayton of the \uc2 Society for Human Resource Management\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "Many of them are reconsidering their policies and saying, 'OK, what can we do to not only retain the people that we have but also to try to recruit more people?'" says Ms. Clayton. "I do think that there are going to be employers that are going to lose staff if they're not flexible."\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Ms. Janin is a Wall Street Journal reporter in New York. She can be reached at alex.janin@wsj.com.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020210923eh9n00001\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
