\par\par \b \uc2 Google\uc2 's Office Purchase Is Priciest Since Pandemic\b0\par\par\uc2 By Konrad Putzier \par \uc2 960 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 September 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Google\uc2  said it is buying a New York City office building for $2.1 billion, one of the clearest signals yet of big technology companies' growing appetite for office space, even as these firms embrace remote work.\par \par \uc2 The deal for the new building on Manhattan's West Side is the most expensive sale of a single U.S. office building since the start of the pandemic -- and one of the priciest in U.S. history, according to data company \uc2 Real Capital Analytics\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Google\uc2 's purchase of the 1.3 million-square-foot waterfront property is the latest sign that fears of the office building's demise look overblown. Vacancy rates across the U.S. are well above pre-pandemic levels and office rents have declined. But in recent weeks there is evidence that cheaper space is luring back corporate tenants, which have been shrugging off delays in returning to the office because of the Delta variant and are planning for the years ahead.\par \par \uc2 In Manhattan, office asking rents are down to where they were in 2017, but August leasing activity for Midtown was more than double the previous month, according to real-estate firm Colliers. French bank \uc2 Credit Agricole SA\uc2  signed a new lease in Midtown, while swimwear maker Longevity Brands added space in the Garment District.\par \par \uc2 Newer office buildings, meanwhile, are finding buyers again after a stretch last year when these properties mostly repelled investors. Towers with tenants committed to long-term leases have sold at high prices, in part because low interest rates make them look like attractive investments in comparison. In March, private-equity firm \uc2 KKR & Co\uc2 . paid $1.1 billion for a San Francisco office building leased to \uc2 Dropbox Inc\uc2 ., the most expensive office sale in the city in more than a decade.\par \par \uc2 Google\uc2 , \uc2 Facebook Inc\uc2 ., \uc2 Apple Inc\uc2 ., \uc2 Amazon.com Inc\uc2 . and other tech giants have emerged in recent years as some of the biggest renters and buyers of office space throughout the U.S. Tech companies are taking advantage of lower office building prices and putting billions of dollars in cash reserves to use, real-estate investors said.\par \par \uc2 "Even if they have 20% of their workforce working remotely, business is just growing so fast that the other 80% still needs a place to go," said \uc2 Brian Kingston\uc2 , chief executive of landlord Brookfield Property Partners.\par \par \uc2 Cities usually welcome the arrival of big tech companies for bringing an influx of well-paid employees and a boost to property-tax revenue, though these companies' presence has also fueled concerns over rising commercial and apartment rents.\par \par \uc2 Google\uc2  has embraced remote work more aggressively than many large-company peers, letting employees work at home since the spring of 2020. \uc2 Sundar Pichai\uc2 , chief executive of Google parent Alphabet Inc., said last month that the company would delay its return to the office until January. But the Manhattan office purchase underscores how the company views office space as an important part of its long-term strategy.\par \par \uc2 Google\uc2 , like many of the country's bigger employers, has said it envisions a hybrid workplace. Mr. Pichai told employees that most of them would end up spending around three days a week in the office and two days working remotely, according to a May email he wrote to employees that \uc2 Google\uc2  published.\par \par \uc2 "We know that our employees, in order to really be happy and productive, need to collaborate," said William Floyd, Google's director of public policy and government affairs. "Because of that need to collaborate, we've been investing more and more in office space."\par \par \uc2 Google\uc2  is already leasing the building, a former freight terminal dubbed St. John's Terminal, in the Hudson Square neighborhood. The company has an option to buy, which it said it plans to exercise in the first quarter of 2022. The Manhattan building is currently under construction and expected to open in mid-2023.\par \par \uc2 Google\uc2  often prefers to buy buildings because owning spaces makes changing them in the future easier, like reconfiguring floor plans. "We like to control our own space," Mr. Floyd said.\par \par \uc2 The sellers are two Canadian companies: real-estate firm \uc2 Oxford Properties Group\uc2 , which joined with Related Cos. on Manhattan's Hudson Yards commercial and residential development, and CPP Investments, a pension fund manager.\par \par \uc2 Google\uc2  has been expanding its New York offices in recent years and now employs around 12,000 people in the city -- its biggest workforce outside California. That number is up from around 7,000 in 2018, Mr. Floyd said. The company's offices, some owned and some leased, are clustered in Manhattan's Chelsea and Hudson Square neighborhoods.\par \par \uc2 The acquisition of St. John's Terminal cements \uc2 Google\uc2 's status as one of Manhattan's biggest commercial property owners. The company paid $2.4 billion for the nearby Chelsea Market building in 2018 and owns 111 Eighth Ave. and the Milk Building at 450 W. 15th St. Globally, Alphabet held $55.9 billion worth of land and buildings as of June 30, according to its latest quarterly filing with the \uc2 Securities and Exchange Commission\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Tech companies have said they favor New York over cheaper cities because of its large skilled labor pool and appeal to many young professionals. \uc2 Amazon.com\uc2  once planned to open a second headquarters in the city but backed out of the plans in 2019 after the subsidies it would receive drew opposition from local lawmakers. \uc2 Google\uc2 's latest deal shows that the \uc2 Amazon\uc2  episode has done little to dent New York's appeal to tech companies.\par \par \uc2 "New York City is the place for world-class talent and \uc2 Google\uc2 's acquisition of 550 Washington Street in Manhattan for its workforce demonstrates that," said Manhattan Borough President Gale Brewer, a Democrat.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw262\pich368\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3930\pichgoal5520
