\par\par \uc2 REVIEW & OUTLOOK (Editorial)\par \b \uc2 The Government Family Plan\b0\par\par\uc2 852 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 September 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A16\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Democrats are back in Washington this week to jam through their $3.5 trillion reconciliation bill within days, including a radical government incursion into American work and family life. With the stakes so high, we'll devote the coming days to telling you more about these vast new programs.\par \par \uc2 Start with the new entitlement for paid leave. The Ways and Means Committee bill tasks the Treasury Department with setting up a program to pay up to 12 weeks of leave for all workers, including the self-employed. This is not a maternity benefit, but a broad program for both sexes to care for new children or an ailing relative "by blood or affinity." Benefits are supposed to replace about two-thirds of wages on average.\par \par \uc2 A White House fact sheet laments that "nearly four of five private sector workers have no access to paid leave." But this \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2  factoid doesn't count paid time off, disability insurance or other benefits. A 2017 \uc2 Pew Research\uc2  survey found that roughly two-thirds of workers who took family or medical leave in the previous two years received at least some pay. Some 55% of companies reported offering paid maternity leave in a 2020 survey by the \uc2 Society for Human Resource Management\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Democrats say their bill will ensure that everyone is covered, especially low-income workers. But workers who live from one paycheck to the next can't afford to take time off for a partial wage replacement, even the 85% of the first $290 in weekly wages that Democrats promise. The Senate's reconciliation rules likely won't let Democrats extend job protection to workers who take the leave, rendering low-income employees even less likely to sign up.\par \par \uc2 The dirty secret is that government leave programs end up helping middle-income folks who can live on partial pay. A 2013 analysis out of California, which offers a state benefit, found that less than 4% of claimants earned less than $12,000; more than 20% earned north of $84,000. Based on the Ways and Means bill's formula, a new parent earning $200,000 a year could be eligible for more than $1,000 a week for 12 weeks every year. No matter if this person is married to another six-figure earner, who can also claim the leave.\par \par \uc2 More astonishing: Democrats want government to take over benefits offered in the private economy. Treasury can reimburse employers who furnish comprehensive paid leave up to 90% of average costs. So taxpayers will subsidize time off for management consultants, accountants and other well-paid professionals.\par \par \uc2 The is all the more regrettable because employers have been racing to expand paid leave, including for hourly workers. Paid leave can reduce turnover, and companies have an incentive to offer it. No company will start a private program if this bill passes. Some may shunt workers onto the federal program and then top off their wages. States that have set up their own programs (New Jersey, for instance) can also be reimbursed -- and will surely use the payroll taxes they levied to finance other spending.\par \par \uc2 By the way, this will be a compliance and administrative nightmare. Treasury will have a mere two years to stand up the program, complete with wage verification and an appeals process. Small businesses are offered grants to "help with costs associated with filling in for an employee" on leave, throwing money at the labor-market disruption Democrats know they are creating.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 This all adds up to an enormous new unfunded entitlement, even before inevitable expansions. An earlier iteration, the Family Act, would have cost more than $540 billion over a decade, according to the \uc2 Congressional Budget Office\uc2 . That bill at least had the honesty to propose a new 0.4% payroll tax, even if it still didn't cover all costs. Democrats now peddle the fiction that workers will never pay for this unrepealable entitlement.\par \par \uc2 In the coming days you will hear that the U.S. is the only developed country that doesn't provide paid leave. What won't be mentioned: These policies haven't delivered on promises like increasing birth rates or narrowing career earnings gaps between men and women. Or that they are funded with steep taxes on middle earners and value-added taxes that slam the poor.\par \par \uc2 Proponents also claim the program will make workers more attached to the labor force. Yet the bill doesn't even stipulate that workers must be currently employed to receive benefits; they only need a de minimis wage history. Democrats in committee rejected a GOP amendment to require more work history.\par \par \uc2 No one wants to force low-income women to return to work two weeks after giving birth, and many voters will assume Democrats are merely expanding the social safety net. But Americans will pay for this entitlement for decades, as Democrats assume even more core responsibilities of the private economy and make the middle class even more dependent on government.\par \par \uc2 (See related letter: "Letters to the Editor: Insurance CEO Defends Paid-Leave Program" -- WSJ Oct. 2, 2021)\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020210921eh9l0000i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
