\par\par \b \uc2 Covid-19 Made Me More Productive\b0\par\par\uc2 By Bob Brody \par \uc2 468 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 September 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A15\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 One effect of the pandemic came as a surprise: My work ethic improved.\par \par \uc2 I wasn't alone. Productivity rose 3.8% over the past four quarters, according to the \uc2 U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2 , while hours worked jumped 13.7%. A \uc2 Harvard Business School\uc2  study showed that the average workday in 16 cities expanded by 48.5 minutes, with employees sending 5.2% more emails and attending 13% more meetings.\par \par \uc2 I worked harder and more productively over the past 18 months than ever, more or less from sunrise to sunset. From my hours at the keyboard my neck, shoulders, back, forearms, wrists and fingers ached.\par \par \uc2 Months before Covid struck, I started my own consulting business. I often lay sleepless at night, feeling overwhelmed by my growing responsibilities. But my industriousness went beyond the desire to establish my company or any transition to telecommuting, since I'd worked remotely for the past 12 years.\par \par \uc2 Rather, it came largely from my precarious state of mind. I was scared -- scared of catching Covid-19, of getting intubated in an overcrowded intensive-care unit, of dying behind a plastic sheet, cut off from my wife, children and granddaughter. I was paranoid, dreading a danger that lurked everywhere, even just outside my front door, and could infect me, my family and my friends.\par \par \uc2 This barely suppressed hysteria gave me a rationale for buckling down: a desperate wish to focus on something else. The world was coming apart, and I wanted none of it. And so I pushed myself extra hard to gain some sense of control. As Samuel Johnson said, "When a man knows he is to be hanged in a fortnight, it concentrates his mind wonderfully."\par \par \uc2 I had played this mental trick on myself at least twice before: after the 9/11 attacks in 2001 and then again after the financial meltdown in 2008. I also occasionally adopted this version of self-therapy -- psychologists label it "avoidant behavior" -- to escape from unappealing professional and social obligations.\par \par \uc2 Sometimes nothing serves us better in moments of crisis than healthy doses of denial and delusion. And the spread of Covid-19 offered opportunity for plenty of both. It would enable me to ward off anxiety, depression and panic. If I worked harder, I reasoned, I could better pretend the whole nightmare would just go away.\par \par \uc2 "We work in the dark -- we do what we can -- we give what we have," the novelist Henry James once wrote. Strange as it sounds, I got lucky in the pandemic. I lost myself in my labors. Work kept me going. As much as anything, it got me through.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Mr. Brody, a consultant and essayist, is the author of "Playing Catch With Strangers: A Family Guy (Reluctantly) Comes of Age."\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020210921eh9l0000q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
