\par\par \b \uc2 REVIEW --- Word on the Street: From Childish Chatter to Sinister Supervision\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ben Zimmer \par \uc2 581 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 September 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par C3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 [Tattleware]\par \par \uc2 "Bosses turn to 'tattleware' to keep tabs on employees working from home," warned a widely shared article published earlier this week in \uc2 the Guardian\uc2 . As the report details with alarm, the pandemic has seen a boom in surveillance software that allows supervisors to monitor workers' every move, logging webcam screenshots, keystrokes, and browsing history.\par \par \uc2 The programs are known collectively as "bossware" or, more commonly, "tattleware" -- a moniker that has struck some observers as a bit too frivolous, considering the serious privacy concerns surrounding such workplace surveillance. If "tattleware" is the label that catches on, it will represent a 21st-century spin on a term long associated with revealing other people's secrets or private affairs.\par \par \uc2 The word "tattle" likely came into English from medieval Flemish, where "tatelen" was a verb meaning "to stammer." "Tatelen" is historically related to a parallel form, "tateren," in languages like Dutch, German, and Frisian, meaning "to chatter," possibly imitating the sound of a baby or a cackling animal.\par \par \uc2 William Caxton, who is believed to have introduced the printing press to England, translated a Dutch allegorical tale called "Reynard the Fox" in 1481, and rendered "tatelen" in English as "tatelyng." For Caxton, "tattling" meant speaking in a faltering or childlike fashion, but it was soon extended to various kinds of idle chitchat.\par \par \uc2 Starting in the 16th century, "tattling" was often associated with promoting a stereotype of talkative women. When King Edward VI, son of Henry VIII, died in 1553 at the tender age of 15, rumors spread throughout Europe that he had been poisoned. An English courtier debunked the rumor by calling it a "slanderous fable" that could only have circulated among "tattling women" and "foolish children."\par \par \uc2 "Tattlers" make an appearance in the King James Bible of 1611, in the First Epistle of Paul to Timothy, containing instructions about the role of women in the church. Widows, it is said, may "learn to be idle . . . and not only idle, but tattlers also and busybodies, speaking things which they ought not."\par \par \uc2 The figure of the "tattler" wasn't always so heavily gendered. From 1709 to 1711, Richard Steele published a notorious journal called "The Tatler," featuring chatty essays by the likes of Joseph Addison and Jonathan Swift writing under pen names, reporting on the society gossip one might hear in London coffeehouses.\par \par \uc2 In American usage in the 19th century, the "tattler" took on a new name: the "tattletale," influenced by the similar word "telltale." In one 1855 story, a mischievous boy bullies his companions into keeping a secret: "Just mind you keep your tongue between your teeth -- you know what happens to tattletales."\par \par \uc2 Among children, "tattling" on someone came to mean reporting on their wrongdoing to an authority figure to get them into trouble. This juvenile connotation helps explain why "tattleware" might seem too unserious as a name for surveillance software.\par \par \uc2 Taking on the "-ware" suffix of "software," "tattleware" follows in the footsteps of such tech terms as "freeware," "shareware," "malware," and "ransomware." The most relevant precursor is "spyware" for software that covertly transmits information about a user's activity. As early as 1994, a participant in an online computing forum asked for information about "spyware," particularly tools enabling an employer "to measure the productiveness of his employees" by spying on them. The latest generation of "tattleware" provides exactly that service, but monitored workers may bristle at the snooping of such digital tattlers.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020210911eh9b0000q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
