\par\par \uc2 PERSONAL JOURNAL\par \b \uc2 Anatomy of a Workout: Four Ways To Stay Fit And Flexible\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jen Murphy \par \uc2 1,379 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 September 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A12\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Working from home has provided many people more time and flexibility to exercise. They can do crunches in their living room between Zoom calls or take conference calls while walking around the block.\par \par \uc2 Employees transitioning to new work schedules might struggle to figure out when to fit in a workout now. Long commutes, gym closures and unexpected meetings are among the obstacles that can derail a workout plan.\par \par \uc2 Anna Baggett, a health coach at Massachusetts General Hospital in Revere, Mass., says the key to adopting a new routine is setting achievable goals and managing expectations. A change in work schedules is often stressful, she says. Trying to find time to exercise shouldn't add to that stress.\par \par \uc2 "If you used to exercise an hour a day and now only have 30 minutes, celebrate the fact that you are doing 30 minutes rather than nothing at all," she says. "And find ways to get the most out of that 30 minutes."\par \par \uc2 NiCole Keith, the Indianapolis-based former president of the American College of Sports Medicine, says scheduling workouts on your calendar and having a workout buddy can help you stay accountable. And if your workout gets sabotaged by a last-minute meeting, try to fit some other form of activity into your day, even if it is 10 or 15 minutes of movement.\par \par \uc2 "You can't think of exercise as selfish," she says. "You need to think of it as being necessary for longevity."\par \par \uc2 Here, experts share advice for readjusting your routine under four different circumstances:\par \par \uc2 -- I'm back on the road for business travel and want to work out whether the gym is open or not.\par \par \uc2 While body-weight exercises such as push-ups, burpees and squats are great, those traveling for extended periods need to maintain a resistance-based strength program, says Vanessa Martin, a trainer and founder of New York-based SIN (Strength in Numbers) Workouts.\par \par \uc2 If you have room, she suggests packing one or more of the following: a jump rope, resistance bands, speed ladder and TRX suspension training kit. If you have to make do with what is in your hotel room, here are Ms. Martin's equipment hacks.\par \par \uc2 Hand Towels or Cloth Napkins\par \par \uc2 "I've had incredible success using these as gliders beneath my feet or hands to perform movements you would usually do with gliders or on a Pilates reformer," she says. "They work best on hardwood surfaces. If carpet is your only option, use paper plates." She suggests putting towels under your hands while holding the plank pose and alternating sliding your hands forward.\par \par \uc2 Wine or Water Bottles\par \par \uc2 A hotel room's minibar doesn't have to be a vice, Ms. Martin says. "I encourage clients looking to maintain their toning and sculpting programs to grab wine bottles and use them as hand weights," she says.\par \par \uc2 Luggage\par \par \uc2 A suitcase, packed or empty, is a great option for adding weight to a lower-body program. "Hold your suitcase just as you would a weight while performing a squat," she says. If you want to work your upper body, you can hold the suitcase handle and perform bent-over rows, biceps curls and overhead presses, she says. Or lie down on the ground and use your suitcase to do a chest press.\par \par \uc2 -- I used to have an hour to work out. My commute cut that to 30 minutes.\par \par \uc2 This body-weight workout from Caitlin Pittaway, trainer and co-owner of Compass Fitness in Denver, can take 25 to 45 minutes depending on how many exercises you choose to tack on.\par \par \uc2 "You can adjust it based on however much time you have that day," she says.\par \par \uc2 First Round*\par \par \uc2 50 Squat Jumps\par \par \uc2 Stand with feet shoulder-width apart and arms overhead. Lower into a squat. Swing the arms forward and up as you explode off the ground. Land in a squat.\par \par \uc2 40 Plank Jacks\par \par \uc2 Start in a high plank pose. Jump your feet out just beyond hip width, then jump them back together.\par \par \uc2 30 Jumping Lunges\par \par \uc2 Start in a split-stance with your right foot forward. Lower until your right knee and back leg form 90-degree angles in a lunge. Jump up and bring your left foot forward and right foot back to lower into a lunge on the opposite side.\par \par \uc2 20 Sit-Ups\par \par \uc2 10 Plank Pop-Ups\par \par \uc2 Start in high plank. Jump your feet forward as you raise your hands and head up. You should land in a low squat, with the weight on your heels. Put your hands on the floor and jump your feet back so you are in a high plank.\par \par \uc2 Second Round**\par \par \uc2 20 Skaters\par \par \uc2 Stand on one leg with hips and knees slightly bent. Perform a dynamic sideways jumping motion. Absorb the impact of the jump with the second leg by landing softly and holding that landing for a few seconds. Then jump off that leg in the opposite direction.\par \par \uc2 15 Squat Jacks\par \par \uc2 Start in a squat position. Jump your feet together and back out just beyond hip width.\par \par \uc2 10 Step-Ups\par \par \uc2 Use a stair or aerobic step, or march in place with high knees.\par \par \uc2 Five Burpees\par \par \uc2 Stand with feet shoulder-width apart. Squat and place your hands on the floor, jump your feet back and perform a push-up, jump forward, then jump up to stand.\par \par \uc2 -- I sit all day at my desk without much chance to move.\par \par \uc2 If you have a desk job, it is even more important for you to sneak bursts of physical activity into your day, says Tom Barcia, a physical therapist and athletic trainer at SPEAR Physical Therapy in New York City.\par \par \uc2 You don't need to do a high-intensity desk workout in office clothes, he says. Simply embracing active alternatives such as taking the stairs or standing instead of sitting in meetings make a difference.\par \par \uc2 Thoracic Extensions\par \par \uc2 Sit in your chair with your feet flat on the ground, spine tall. Loosely lace your hands at the bottom of your head for support. Slowly arch the upper back and neck backward. Avoid arching in the low back. You should feel an opening through your chest that counteracts the forward, rounded position many of us hold while working at a computer. Perform two sets of 10 repetitions.\par \par \uc2 Squat-With-Chair Touch\par \par \uc2 Stand in front of your chair with your feet shoulder-width apart, hands clasped in front of you. Sit your hips back as if you were going to sit into the chair. Gently tap the chair with your butt, then stand and repeat. Complete three sets of eight to 10 repetitions.\par \par \uc2 Scapular Retractions\par \par \uc2 Sit tall in your chair. Gently squeeze your shoulder blades together, hold for three seconds, relax and repeat. Make sure to maintain good posture. Perform two sets of 10 repetitions, or any time you catch yourself slouching.\par \par \uc2 -- Evenings are my only opportunity to exercise but I'm tired when I get home.\par \par \uc2 Plan ahead to get active when you return home. Dr. Keith suggests putting on your workout clothes before you leave the office.\par \par \uc2 At day's end, it also helps if you can make exercise enjoyable. "You could watch sports or the news on the couch or you could get in a workout in front of the TV," she says.\par \par \uc2 If you are tired, do something easy like walking, suggests Alecia Gende, a member of the sports-medicine team at the Mayo Clinic in Onalaska, Wis.\par \par \uc2 *First Round: After you finish the five exercises in the first round, set your timer for 10 minutes. Hold a high plank or low plank (from your forearms) for as long as you can.\par \par \uc2 When you can't hold your plank any longer with proper form -- lifting or sinking your hips counts as breaking -- pause your timer and complete the last four exercises.\par \par \uc2 **Second Round: When you have completed the four exercises in the second round, drop back into a plank and restart your timer where you left off.\par \par \uc2 If you need to modify to maintain form, drop to your knees. As soon as your form breaks, do the four drills again, then drop into plank and reset your timer.\par \par \uc2 Keep repeating until you reach 10 minutes.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020210907eh9700015\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
