\par\par \b \uc2 Snack Shortage Looms With Mondelez Strike\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jesse Newman and Jaewon Kang \par \uc2 952 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 September 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 U.S. grocers are bracing for potential snack food disruptions, as a weekslong strike at \uc2 Mondelez International Inc\uc2 . facilities prompts fears of shortages in Ritz crackers and Premium saltines.\par \par \uc2 Grocers across the country are boosting orders of Ritz crackers and stocking up on alternatives in case the strike, now stretching into its fourth week, slows production of the crackers or cookies such as Oreos or Chips Ahoy.\par \par \uc2 The strike, kicked off Aug. 10 by unionized workers at a Mondelez bakery in Portland, Ore., has since spread to two other bakeries and three distribution facilities in Colorado, Illinois, Georgia and Virginia. They are opposing changes to work schedules, overtime and healthcare sought by Mondelez in contract negotiations.\par \par \uc2 Disruptions to snack production stemming from the strike would mark the latest supply challenge for food sellers since the start of the pandemic last year. Shortages of labor and raw materials in recent months have left retailers short on everything from packaged beverages such as Capri Sun to pet food.\par \par \uc2 Unionized employees are pushing back against Mondelez proposals that workers see lengthening shifts while cutting into their ability to earn overtime pay, a large portion of many workers' annual incomes.\par \par \uc2 The company said it is trying to respond to strong demand for some of its products and address disruptions caused by worker absences.\par \par \uc2 Employees are on strike at three of the four U.S. bakeries Mondelez owns. The company says operations are continuing at its affected facilities, which have enacted preformulated plans to keep production lines running. Salaried employees have stepped in to help at some sites and third-party manufacturers also are working to keep customers' orders filled, said Laurie Guzzinati, a Mondelez spokeswoman.\par \par \uc2 "It's business as usual as we focus on continuing to meet the needs of our customers," Ms. Guzzinati said. The company says its other manufacturing and distribution facilities are fully operational and that its sales teams are in close touch with customers.\par \par \uc2 Tops Markets LLC\uc2  and other grocers said they are ordering more snacks from Mondelez and other suppliers after Mondelez notified them of the strike. Nebraska-based \uc2 B&R Stores Inc\uc2 . has been preparing for possible shortages, canceling discounts on Ritz and Mondelez's other Nabisco-brand crackers to manage supply, President Mark Griffin said. B&R is also ordering more Club and Townhouse crackers from rival \uc2 Kellogg Co\uc2 .'s Keebler brand.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Griffin said he is most concerned about supplies of crackers such as Ritz and Premium saltines, which he said Mondelez told him are mostly produced in the U.S. There are few alternatives to Mondelez snacks, so it can be tough to secure inventory ahead of the holidays when Ritz and Triscuits crackers are big sellers, he added.\par \par \uc2 "We are trying to get on it immediately and load on it as much as we can now," Mr. Griffin said.\par \par \uc2 Grocery executives said supplies of Mondelez cookies and crackers largely held up during the pandemic, but they worry they will become tougher to keep in stock as supply challenges compound. Executives say they are encountering new inventory problems weekly in many types of grocery items, as well as ebbs and flows in product availability.\par \par \uc2 It is difficult to decide how many more crackers and cookies to procure, executives said. Keith Milligan, controller of Piggly Wiggly stores in Alabama and Georgia, said he is afraid to place big orders for Kellogg products because Mondelez items sell better in his stores.\par \par \uc2 "We may end up with too many," Mr. Milligan said.\par \par \uc2 Mondelez and representatives from the Bakery, Confectionary, Tobacco Workers and Grain Millers union have been negotiating since contracts expired in May. In a July proposal, the company sought to establish 12-hour shifts for employees working on select, high-demand lines. Weekend shifts would garner overtime pay only if employees work their scheduled Monday through Friday shifts, and future employees would be required to contribute to their healthcare plans.\par \par \uc2 Union leaders calculate the changes could cost employees as much as $40,000 per year in overtime pay, said Darlene Carpenter, a business agent for the local chapter of the bakery workers' union in Richmond, Va. She said that isn't sitting well with employees who worked 16-hour shifts or as many as 20 days straight during the pandemic, when demand for snack food soared, boosting revenue for Mondelez.\par \par \uc2 "We want to be able to support our families and send our kids to college also," Ms. Carpenter said.\par \par \uc2 Mondelez said its employees are well-compensated and still will have opportunities to receive overtime pay.\par \par \uc2 Union leaders in Oregon, Illinois and Virginia say they believe the strike, which involves more than 1,000 workers, already has disrupted bakery production.\par \par \uc2 Cameron Taylor, a business agent for the local union chapter in Portland, Ore., said workers there joined the strike from their posts at ovens and along production lines, leaving products stranded on conveyor belts. Mechanics and electricians belonging to other unions have also joined in a show of solidarity, said Mr. Taylor, a bakery veteran of 14 years.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Taylor said employees picketing near railroad tracks that lead to the Portland bakery had disrupted shipments of ingredients like flour and sugar to the plant.\par \par \uc2 He said: "It looks pretty deserted."\par \par \uc2 Mondelez said it is working with suppliers to ensure deliveries and that manufacturing continues at the site.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Corrections & Amplifications\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 The Bakery, Confectionery, Tobacco Workers and Grain Millers International Union\uc2  was incorrectly referred to as the Bakery, Confectionary, Tobacco Workers and Grain Millers union in a Business & Finance article on Friday about the impact on snack supplies from a strike at \uc2 Mondelez International Inc\uc2 . facilities.\par \par \uc2 (WSJ September 7, 2021)\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020210903eh930001l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
