\par\par \uc2 Heard on the Street\par \b \uc2 Zoom's Growth Outlook Is Muted\b0\par\par\uc2 By Laura Forman \par \uc2 627 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 September 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B14\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Corrections & Amplifications\par \par \uc2 Zoom Video Communications Inc. went public in the spring of 2019. A Heard on the Street column on Sept. 1 about the company incorrectly said the initial public offering was roughly 18 months ago.\par \par \uc2 (WSJ Sept. 8, 2021)\par \par \uc2 (END)\par \par \uc2 [Financial Analysis and Commentary]\par \par \uc2 Full-time remote work won't stick just because some of us want it to.\par \par \uc2 Zoom Video Communications\uc2 ' latest results are a case in point. While revenue for the videoconferencing company's quarter ended July 31 came in just above Wall Street's estimate, Zoom is no longer selling itself. Research-and-development costs nearly doubled in the quarter from the year-earlier period, while sales and marketing as a percentage of revenue increased. Despite the investments, the company's guidance implied revenue growth will significantly decelerate throughout the year. Shares of Zoom fell more than 16% on Tuesday following Monday's earnings report.\par \par \uc2 If you are planning on continuing to work from home every day for the foreseeable future, chances are that is a luxurious choice. Companies are beginning to require their employees back in the office some or all of the time. A working paper published in April by the \uc2 National Bureau of Economic Research\uc2  predicts 20% of full workdays will be supplied from home after the pandemic ends, up from 5% beforehand.\par \par \uc2 Assuming a given 52-week year offers 260 total weekdays, that works out to a roughly 300% increase in work days done at home -- a figure that matters as investors try to value Zoom's shares while it aims to go from a pandemic workaround to a work staple. Heading into Monday's report, Zoom's shares were up 3% this year, which may appear modest given the hybrid work models many companies are pursuing.\par \par \uc2 But Zoom shares have returned more than 800% in the roughly 18 months from when they first started trading in the spring of 2019. Shares have come off their highs as vaccines were rolled out, but they are trading more than 360% above where they closed in their trading debut.\par \par \uc2 Because business from small companies has been volatile as economies reopen, investors have turned their focus toward the longer-term opportunity of in-office tools. With these newer products like Zoom Phone, meant to replace office phones, and Zoom Rooms for enhanced conference rooms, Zoom is trying to sell consistency to companies in what is currently a highly inconsistent world.\par \par \uc2 It is a value proposition that has been resonating lately -- at least among its existing customers. Back in June, Zoom said it took seven quarters to sell its first million Phone seats. It now says it will have reached two million Phone seats in August, adding roughly half a million seats in just four months.\par \par \uc2 The purchase of Zoom's in-office tools are corporate-level decisions, the likes of which are being made in size right now as companies roll out their post-Covid work plans. The question is where that growth will shake out longer-term, especially relative to competitors like \uc2 Google\uc2  and \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 . Zoom continues to show strong increases in user adoption and in purchases of additional products among its existing customers. But the company added less than 8,000 customers with over 10 employees in the quarter ended July 31 -- down from about 30,000 customers of that size in the previous period.\par \par \uc2 Zoom ended the last quarter of its last fiscal year growing revenue more than 360%. The midpoint of its new forecast implies it will end the same period of this fiscal year growing revenue under 20%.\par \par \uc2 At the end of the day, those are numbers that even the best video technology can't amplify.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw262\pich376\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3930\pichgoal5640
